export const HVAC_MODULE_CONTENT = {
  1: {
    "Supervisor Certificate Classes & Scope": `Denver Community Planning & Development (CPD) issues mechanical supervisor certificates by class. Picking the correct class is the very first step — every other decision (experience documentation, exam number, references you carry into the testing center) flows from it. Your friend pulling permits on a Denver hotel needs the broadest scope, which is **Class A Heating & Ventilating Supervisor**: any size building, any occupancy, including high-rise. **Class B (Residential)** is restricted to **one- and two-family dwellings, IRC townhomes, and Group U** occupancies with A/C limited to **5 tons** and water heaters to **100 MBtu** input — useless for a hotel build. **Refrigeration Class A/B** mirrors the H&V classes for refrigeration systems, cooling towers, pipe insulation, and low-voltage controls. **Steam & Hot Water** and **Gas Service Contractor** are separate certs with their own exams.

Each class is a separate certificate even though the underlying ICC exam often qualifies you for multiple. Passing **ICC F29 (or G29 current edition)** qualifies an applicant for Heating & Ventilating Class A, Refrigeration Class A, Steam & Hot Water, and Boilermaker — but you must apply separately for each cert you want issued. The **Gas Service Contractor Supervisor** cert requires **ICC F33 (Master Gas Pipefitter)** and is the only one that covers commercial gas piping, controls, commercial cooking equipment, and water heaters **≤100 MBtu**. All supervisor certificates renew every **3 years**.

**Exam Trap:** Students assume one exam = one license. It does not. ICC F29 qualifies you for **four** supervisor classes, but Denver CPD issues each one only if you apply and pay for it. Showing up at a hotel job site with only a Refrigeration Class A cert when the scope includes ventilation is a permit-pulling failure even if you passed the exam years ago.

**Exam Trap:** Class B scope sounds residential but the **100 MBtu water heater limit** and **5-ton A/C limit** are absolute caps — not "typical." A 6-ton split system in a single-family home requires a Class A supervisor to pull the permit, not Class B.`,

    "Experience & Qualifying Requirements": `Denver CPD requires documented experience in addition to passing the exam. For **Class A Heating & Ventilating Supervisor**, the path is **8 years total**: **4 years journeyman H&V** plus **4 years as designer, draftsman, or supervisor**. An alternate path allows **4 years field installation (apprenticeship or OJT with a minimum of 7,000 hours)** plus **2 years designer/supervisor**. Either way, **EPA Section 608 certification** must be in hand before the Class A application is approved — Denver will not issue the cert without it. **Class B (Residential)** is shorter: **6 years total — 4 years journeyman + 2 years designer/supervisor**.

**Refrigeration Class A/B** uses the same experience tiers as H&V A/B. **Steam & Hot Water Supervisor** demands the full **8-year Class A tier**. **Gas Service Contractor Supervisor** is **6 years — 4 years field + 2 years designer/supervisor**. Experience is documented via affidavits from prior employers stating dates, hours, and scope of work performed. Self-employed experience requires tax records, permit history, or other third-party verification — Denver does not accept self-certified hours. Apprenticeship hours through a registered program (Joint Apprenticeship Training Committee, etc.) count toward the journeyman portion.

**Exam Trap:** The "designer/draftsman/supervisor" years are NOT the same as more journeyman field years. A candidate with **8 years of pure field installation** does not qualify for Class A — they're missing the **4 years of designer/supervisor experience** the cert requires. The two halves of the experience requirement are distinct and both mandatory.

**Exam Trap:** EPA 608 is a federal cert, not a Denver cert, but Denver **requires proof of 608 certification on file** for the Class A application. Candidates often pass F29 and then get rejected because they assumed they could finish 608 later. Get the 608 Universal cert first, then sit F29.`,

    "Exam Format, Fees, & References": `The **ICC F29 (or G29 current edition) National Standard Master Mechanical** exam is **100 multiple-choice questions** in **4 hours**, **open book**, with a **70% pass score**. Fee is approximately **$155**, paid to ICC, and the exam is delivered at PearsonVUE or PSI test centers. **F32 (Residential)** is **70 questions in 3 hours**, also open book and 70% pass. **F33 (Master Gas Pipefitter)** is approximately **80 questions in 4 hours**. **EPA 608** is delivered as four sections of **25 questions each** (Core + Type I + Type II + Type III); pass score is **18 of 25 (72%)** per section, and **Universal** requires passing each section individually.

Allowed references for F29/F32 are the **IMC and IFGC** in the edition the exam targets (currently 2018 ICC for G29; verify with Denver CPD which edition is currently administered). F33 allows IFGC only. EPA 608 Core is typically **closed-book**; Types I/II/III are typically open-book per the proctor's manual. Tabs and highlighting are usually allowed in code books, but written notes on margins are not — bring clean, indexed code books with stick-on tabs at the key tables (combustion air, duct sizing, gas pipe sizing, dryer exhaust). Calculators must be silent, non-printing, and non-programmable.

**Exam Trap:** "Open book" lulls candidates into under-preparing. At **2 minutes 24 seconds per question** on F29, you will not have time to look up more than ~25 answers — the rest must come from memory. Use the book for tables and numerical lookups, not for definitions or basic rules.

**Exam Trap:** ICC retires and re-numbers exams without much public notice. **F29 referenced 2015 codes**; **G29 references 2018 codes**. Showing up with the wrong code edition is a wasted $155 — your book will not match the questions. Always confirm with Denver CPD and ICC which exam number and edition is currently live before scheduling.`
  },

  2: {
    "Code Structure & Open-Book Strategy": `The IMC and IFGC are organized as separate ICC code books that work together. The **IMC (International Mechanical Code)** has **15 chapters** covering general provisions, definitions, ventilation, exhaust systems, duct systems, combustion air (cross-referenced to IFGC), chimneys/vents, specific appliances, boilers, refrigeration, hydronics, fuel oil, solar, and references. The **IFGC (International Fuel Gas Code)** has **8 chapters** covering general provisions, definitions, gas piping, combustion air, chimneys/vents for gas, specific gas appliances, gaseous hydrogen systems, and referenced standards. The two codes overlap deliberately on chimneys, vents, and combustion air — when a gas appliance is involved, **IFGC governs** even if the IMC has parallel language.

Open-book strategy is built around three habits. First, **tab the key tables before exam day**: IFGC 402.4 (gas pipe sizing), IFGC 304.5 (two-opening combustion air), IMC 403.3.1.1 (outdoor air rates), IMC 504.6.4.1 (dryer exhaust length), IMC 1106 (refrigerant occupancy classifications), and Table 401.5 (outdoor intake separation). Second, **learn the index, not just the table of contents** — most code answers are found faster by searching the index for a keyword (e.g., "dryer," "drip leg," "machinery room") than by scanning chapter headings. Third, **answer all definition and concept questions from memory first**, then return to lookup-required questions in a second pass.

**Exam Trap:** Many candidates flip to the wrong code book. A combustion air question for a **gas furnace** lives in **IFGC Chapter 3**, not IMC Chapter 7. A chimney clearance question for an **oil-fired** appliance lives in IMC Chapter 8. The fuel determines the book. Pick the wrong book and you'll waste 90 seconds before realizing the section numbers don't line up.

**Exam Trap:** "Open book" does not mean "every wrong answer is forgivable." Open-book exams typically have more nuanced, scenario-based questions that test whether you can apply a rule, not just locate it. Memorize the top 20 numbers (clearances, percentages, lengths) so you only need the book for table lookups.`,

    "Key Tables & How to Find Them Fast": `A handful of tables generate a disproportionate share of testable questions. In the IMC, **Table 403.3.1.1** (minimum outdoor air rates per ASHRAE 62.1) drives every ventilation calculation — common values: office at **5 cfm/person + 0.06 cfm/sq ft**, hotel guest rooms at **5 cfm/person + 0.06 cfm/sq ft**, restaurant dining at **7.5 cfm/person + 0.18 cfm/sq ft**, classrooms at **10 cfm/person + 0.12 cfm/sq ft**. **Section 401.4** (prose, not a table) sets outdoor air intake separation — workhorse numbers are **10 feet** from plumbing vents and contaminant sources, **30 feet** from hazardous exhaust outlets. **Table 504.8.4.1** caps dryer exhaust at **35 feet** of total developed length (2018 IMC; older 2015 IMC used §504.6).

In the IFGC, **Table 402.4(2)** (Schedule 40 metallic pipe at 0.5 psig, 0.5" w.c. pressure drop) is the gas-pipe-sizing table most commonly tested — you input pipe length and required cubic feet per hour, and read off pipe diameter. The **combustion air denominators** are the most-confused numbers on the F29/G29 exam — memorize them as a triad: **outdoor two-opening method (IFGC 304.6.1, direct outdoors or vertical duct) = 1 sq inch per 4,000 Btu/hr per opening**; **outdoor two-opening with horizontal duct = 1 sq inch per 2,000 Btu/hr per opening**; **outdoor single-opening method (IFGC 304.6.2) = 1 sq inch per 3,000 Btu/hr**. The **indoor combining-spaces method (IFGC 304.5.3.3)** between adjacent rooms uses **1 sq inch per 1,000 Btu/hr per opening** — DIFFERENT scenario from outdoor air, even though students confuse them constantly. All openings have a **100 sq inch minimum-area floor**. The **all-air-from-inside method (IFGC 304.5.1)** demands room volume **≥ 50 ft³ per 1,000 Btu/hr** of total input — only usable when infiltration is high enough (rare in modern tight construction). Tab every one of these tables and sections before exam day.

**Exam Trap:** Outdoor air rates in Table 403.3.1.1 are given as **two components: cfm/person AND cfm/sq ft**. Both must be calculated and added. Students often use only the per-person value for occupancies they assume are "people-driven" (offices, hotels) — and undersize by **30-40%**. Always add both components.

**Exam Trap:** The gas pipe sizing table you use must match the **pressure and pressure drop** stated in the question. IFGC has multiple tables for different inlet pressures (0.5 psig, 2 psig, 5 psig) and pressure drops (0.3", 0.5", 3.0"). Using a 2 psig table on a 0.5 psig system will give you a pipe size that's too small and the gas appliances will starve.`,

    "IMC vs IFGC vs IBC Authority": `In Colorado and almost every other adopting jurisdiction, the IMC, IFGC, and IBC are **separate code books with overlapping jurisdiction**, and a single project frequently triggers all three. The general rule: **IBC (International Building Code)** controls occupancy classification, fire-rated assemblies, egress, and shaft enclosures; **IMC** controls mechanical systems (ventilation, ducts, exhaust, refrigeration, hydronics); **IFGC** controls gas piping, gas appliance combustion air, and gas appliance venting. Where IMC and IFGC overlap (notably chimneys, vents, and combustion air for gas appliances), **the IFGC governs gas appliances**.

When codes conflict, the **more restrictive provision applies** unless the AHJ rules otherwise. Denver layered amendments on top of the I-Codes: the **2025 Denver Building Code (DBC)** adopts the **2024 IMC and 2024 IFGC** with Denver-specific changes for snow load, freeze protection, and tight residential envelopes. ASHRAE 62.1 is **referenced** by the IMC for ventilation rates — it is not a separately adopted code in most jurisdictions, but the IMC table 403.3.1.1 numbers come directly from it. ASHRAE 15 is referenced by IMC Chapter 11 for refrigeration system safety. NFPA 96 is referenced by IMC Chapter 5 for commercial kitchen hood installations.

**Exam Trap:** Commercial kitchen hood **suppression systems** are governed by **NFPA 96**, not the IMC. The IMC tells you when a Type I hood is required and its dimensional clearances; NFPA 96 tells you about the wet-chemical suppression system, fusible links, and inspection intervals. A question about hood suppression chemicals or activation temperatures is testing NFPA 96 — even if the question doesn't say so.

**Exam Trap:** A gas furnace in a hotel mechanical room is governed by **IFGC for combustion air and venting**, **IMC for duct distribution**, and **IBC for the mechanical room fire rating**. Candidates who try to answer everything from the IMC will miss the combustion-air sizing question entirely — it's only in IFGC Chapter 3.`
  },

  3: {
    "BTU, MBH, Therm, CFM": `These four units are the daily vocabulary of HVAC and every exam question with a number in it assumes you know which unit you're working in. A **BTU (British thermal unit)** is the energy required to raise **1 pound of water 1°F**. **BTU/hr** is the rate of energy transfer — a 60,000 BTU/hr furnace produces that much heat each hour at full fire. **MBH** means **thousand BTU/hr**, so **60 MBH = 60,000 BTU/hr**. Watch the capital M — in HVAC nomenclature it stands for "mille" (thousand), not "mega." A **therm** is **100,000 BTU**, used by gas utilities for billing. One standard cubic foot of natural gas at 1,000 BTU/cf burns to produce approximately **1 therm per 100 cubic feet**.

**CFM (cubic feet per minute)** is volumetric airflow. It shows up everywhere: ventilation rates (5 cfm/person), exhaust fans (50 cfm bathroom intermittent, 20 cfm continuous), mechanical combustion air (**0.35 cfm per 1,000 Btu/hr**), and duct sizing. To convert cfm to BTU/hr of sensible cooling or heating: **BTU/hr (sensible) = 1.08 × cfm × ΔT** where ΔT is the temperature change in °F. For air at standard density, 1 cfm of supply air at a 20°F temperature drop removes roughly **21.6 BTU/hr** of sensible load.

**Key Formula:** Sensible BTU/hr = 1.08 × cfm × ΔT (°F)

**Exam Trap:** **MBH is thousand, not million**. A 200 MBH boiler is 200,000 BTU/hr — not 200,000,000. Students misread MBH as MMBtu (which IS millions) and oversize equipment by a factor of 1,000. If you see MBH on the exam, multiply by 1,000 to get BTU/hr.

**Exam Trap:** Gas appliance input ratings are in **BTU/hr input**, not output. A **100,000 BTU/hr** furnace at **80% AFUE** delivers only **80,000 BTU/hr** of usable heat. Combustion air calculations use **input** (you're sizing for the fuel being burned, not the heat delivered).`,

    "Plenum, Return, Supply, Exhaust": `Air distribution terminology is tested both as definitions and as compliance triggers. **Supply air** is conditioned air delivered to the occupied space from the air handler. **Return air** is air drawn back from the occupied space to the air handler for re-conditioning. **Outside air (OA)** is fresh air brought in from outdoors to dilute contaminants — typically mixed with return air before passing through cooling/heating coils. **Exhaust air** is removed from the building and not returned. **Make-up air (MUA)** is conditioned outside air introduced specifically to replace air being exhausted (e.g., commercial kitchen hood). A **plenum** per IMC 202 is a compartment or chamber to which one or more ducts connect, forming part of the air distribution system, and that is **not used for the housing of personnel**. The space above a suspended ceiling used to return air is a **return-air plenum** if it meets IMC 602 requirements.

Return air restrictions per IMC 601.5 prohibit drawing return air from: closets (except laundry), kitchens, bathrooms, garages, mechanical rooms, refrigeration machinery rooms, and rooms where flammable vapors or hazardous fumes exist. Combustion air for fuel-burning equipment must **not** be combined with the return air path. Materials inside a plenum must comply with IMC 602.2 — combustible material is prohibited except where the material has a **flame spread index of 25 or less and smoke-developed index of 50 or less**.

**Exam Trap:** Drawing return air from a **garage** is one of the most-cited residential mechanical violations. Even if the air handler is in the garage, the **return duct must originate from inside the dwelling envelope** (typically a central return grille in a hallway) — not from the garage itself. This rule prevents pulling CO and gasoline vapors into living spaces.

**Exam Trap:** A **plenum-rated cable** (CMP, MPP) is required for wiring run **inside a return-air plenum**, including the space above a suspended ceiling used for return. Standard PVC-jacketed cable is not permitted in this space. The exam will sometimes describe a "ceiling space" without using the word plenum — if return air uses that space, it's a plenum.`,

    "Appliance Categories I–IV (Venting)": `Gas appliances are classified by **vent category** based on flue gas temperature and pressure, and the category determines what vent material is allowed. **Category I**: negative vent pressure, non-condensing (flue gas above dew point). Almost all conventional atmospheric-draft and natural-draft gas furnaces, water heaters, and boilers are Category I. They vent through **Type B vent** (double-wall metal) or masonry chimneys with proper liner. **Category II**: negative pressure, condensing. Rare in modern equipment. **Category III**: positive vent pressure, non-condensing. Found in some commercial water heaters and unit heaters; requires gas-tight, corrosion-resistant special vent (AL29-4C or stainless). **Category IV**: positive pressure, condensing. Modern high-efficiency furnaces, condensing boilers, tankless water heaters. Vented through **PVC, CPVC, polypropylene, or stainless steel** per the appliance listing.

The category drives clearance and termination. **Type B vent** clearance to combustibles is **1 inch** (or **0** if the listing marks it for zero clearance). **Single-wall metal vent** clearance is **6 inches** to combustibles. **Masonry chimneys** require **2 inches** clearance to combustibles inside the building and **1 inch** exterior. PVC vent for a Category IV appliance must follow the **manufacturer's installation instructions exactly** — never substitute material or fittings, and never mix PVC and CPVC unless the manual permits.

**Exam Trap:** **Category I and IV cannot share a common vent.** A non-condensing atmospheric water heater (Cat I) and a condensing furnace (Cat IV) on the same vent will backdraft the water heater because the condensing appliance lacks the chimney effect to draw the Cat I flue gas up. Combining categories is a code-and-killer mistake — separate vents are required.

**Exam Trap:** **PVC is not "vent pipe."** PVC schedule 40 used as a Category IV vent is approved **only** because the appliance manufacturer specifies it in their installation instructions. The IMC itself does not list PVC as a generic approved vent material — its acceptance comes through the appliance listing. Using PVC on a Category I or III appliance is an immediate violation.`
  },

  4: {
    "Equipment Access & Working Clearances": `IMC 306 governs access and working space for mechanical equipment. Equipment must be installed where it can be accessed for inspection, service, repair, and replacement without removing permanent construction. The general working space rule is **30 inches wide by 30 inches deep with 78 inches of headroom** measured in front of the service side of the equipment. Roof-mounted appliances need a permanent **walkway not less than 24 inches wide** when the equipment is located more than **16 feet above grade**, plus a **service receptacle within 25 feet** of the equipment on the same level and within sight. A **light fixture** must be provided at the equipment with a switch at the access opening if the equipment is in an attic or under-floor space.

Attic-installed appliances per IMC 306.3 require a passageway not less than **30 inches high and 22 inches wide** (or as large as the equipment passing through, whichever is greater), not exceeding **20 feet in length** from the access opening, with a **24 inch wide solid floor** running the entire length of the passageway and around the unit. Equipment in **under-floor spaces (crawl spaces)** per IMC 306.4 requires the same general access — passageway not less than **30 inches high by 22 inches wide**, not over **20 feet long**, with light and switch. Listed clearance to combustibles must always be maintained; the default for unlisted equipment is **6 inches** unless the manufacturer specifies otherwise.

**Exam Trap:** The **30×30×78** working clearance is the **general default**, but **the manufacturer's listed clearance overrides** if it's larger. A boiler labeled "36 inches front service clearance required" must have 36 inches even though the IMC default is 30. Always defer to listed clearance when it exceeds code minimum.

**Exam Trap:** Roof-mounted equipment **above 16 feet** requires the permanent walkway AND the receptacle within 25 feet AND in line-of-sight. Many candidates remember the walkway but forget the receptacle, or remember the receptacle and forget the "within sight" requirement. All three elements are testable.`,

    "Condensate Disposal": `Per IMC 307, condensate from cooling coils, evaporators, and heat exchangers must be drained to an **approved location** — never onto walking surfaces, into electrical equipment, or where it can cause damage. The minimum slope of a condensate drain line is **1/8 inch per foot** toward the discharge. Approved drainage pipe materials are **PVC, CPVC, ABS, polyethylene, copper, and cast iron** — **galvanized steel is not permitted** because condensate is mildly acidic and corrodes galvanized fast. The condensate drain pipe size is determined by Table 307.2.2 based on equipment cooling capacity, but in residential applications **3/4 inch** is the most common minimum.

Suspended air handlers and any equipment located where leakage could cause damage must have an **auxiliary (secondary) drain pan** per IMC 307.2.3, or a means to detect leakage (such as a **float switch** that shuts off the equipment when the primary drain backs up). The auxiliary drain pan must extend at least **3 inches beyond the equipment footprint** on all sides and be a minimum of **1-1/2 inches deep**. The secondary drain line must terminate at a **conspicuous location** so an occupant sees water dripping and calls for service — a common termination is over a bathroom window or above an exterior door. If a float switch is used instead of a secondary pan, no secondary drain line is required.

**Exam Trap:** A **secondary drain or float switch is required for suspended air handlers** even when the building has no finished space below. The trigger is the **potential to cause damage**, which an inspector reads broadly. Don't argue with the inspector — install one or the other.

**Exam Trap:** The secondary drain line **must NOT be tied into the primary** drain. The whole point is that the secondary catches what the primary missed. If you tee the secondary into the primary at any point, both lines will be plugged simultaneously and the system fails as designed. Run the secondary independently to the conspicuous discharge location.`,

    "Appliance Location (Attics, Garages, Closets)": `Where you put the equipment determines what protections and clearances you must add. **Attic installations** per IMC 306.3 require the passageway and service platform described above, plus **adequate combustion air** if a fuel-burning appliance is installed. **Garage installations** per IMC 304 require that any source of ignition (pilot light, igniter, motor) be located at least **18 inches above the garage floor** to keep ignition above the level where heavier-than-air gasoline vapors would accumulate. Direct-vent or flammable vapor ignition-resistant (FVIR) appliances may be exempt from the 18-inch elevation — check the appliance listing. Equipment in a garage must also be protected from vehicle impact by bollards, raised platforms, or location.

**Closet installations** require special attention to **combustion air** because closets are typically tight, small-volume spaces. Direct-vent and sealed-combustion appliances draw combustion air through their own intake from outside and exhaust through their own vent — they do not require additional room-volume combustion air. Atmospheric and natural-draft appliances in closets almost always require **two openings to outdoors** (top and bottom, per IFGC 304.5) sized at **1 sq inch per 1,000 Btu/hr** of total input with each opening a minimum of **100 sq inches**. A water heater in a bedroom or bathroom closet may only be installed if it is **direct-vent or installed in a dedicated enclosure with a solid weatherstripped door and combustion air ducted from outside** (IFGC 303.3).

**Exam Trap:** A **gas water heater in a bedroom or bathroom** is widely misunderstood. It is **not absolutely prohibited** — it is only prohibited unless it is **direct-vent** or housed in a fully enclosed compartment with a tight gasketed door and combustion air ducted from outside. The exam answer is "permitted with conditions," not "prohibited."

**Exam Trap:** The **18-inch ignition source elevation** in garages applies to **fuel-burning appliances with an ignition source** — water heaters, furnaces, boilers. It does **not** apply to electric water heaters or to FVIR-listed appliances. Don't apply the 18-inch rule blindly.`
  },

  5: {
    "Indoor Air Method": `The indoor air method (also called the "standard method" or "all-air-from-inside" method) per IFGC 304.5 allows combustion air to be drawn from inside the building when the building has sufficient volume and air-leakage rate to supply it. The required room volume is **at least 50 cubic feet per 1,000 Btu/hr** of total input rating of all fuel-burning appliances in the space. For a typical **100,000 Btu/hr** residential furnace plus a **40,000 Btu/hr** water heater (total 140,000 Btu/hr), the required volume is **140 × 50 = 7,000 cubic feet** — roughly an **875 square foot room with 8-foot ceilings**, which most mechanical closets do not meet.

The indoor air method assumes **standard building construction with air infiltration of approximately 0.4 air changes per hour**. Modern tight construction (post-1990 with weatherstripping, foam, and tight windows) frequently does NOT meet this assumption — in those buildings, the **known-air-infiltration-rate (KAIR) method** or **outdoor air method** must be used instead. When adjoining rooms are used to combine volume, the rooms must communicate through **permanent openings** (not doors that can be closed), and each opening must be at least the cross-sectional area required if all the volume came from one room.

**Key Formula:** Indoor Air Method volume required = **(Total appliance input in Btu/hr) ÷ 1,000 × 50 ft³**

**Exam Trap:** The 50 ft³/1,000 Btu/hr rule is calculated on **total combined input** of all appliances in the space. Don't size for the furnace alone if a water heater is in the same closet — add their inputs first, then multiply by 50.

**Exam Trap:** The indoor air method **cannot be used in tight construction** unless the actual infiltration rate is known and the KAIR calculation is performed. Denver's tight-envelope residential code amendments push almost all new-construction combustion air calculations to the outdoor methods. On the exam, assume tight construction unless told otherwise.`,

    "Outdoor Air — Two-Opening & One-Opening": `When a mechanical room cannot supply combustion air from indoor volume, IFGC 304.6 governs **outdoor air** supply. The denominators here are the most common exam-day arithmetic mistakes — internalize this table before anything else.

| Method | Per opening | Minimum |
|---|---|---|
| **Two-opening, direct outdoors or vertical duct** (IFGC 304.6.1) | **1 sq in per 4,000 Btu/hr** of total input | **100 sq in** each |
| **Two-opening, horizontal duct** (IFGC 304.6.1) | **1 sq in per 2,000 Btu/hr** of total input | 100 sq in each |
| **One-opening, direct outdoors** (IFGC 304.6.2) | **1 sq in per 3,000 Btu/hr** of total input | ≥ flue area of largest appliance |
| Indoor combining-spaces (between rooms, NOT to outdoors — IFGC 304.5.3) | **1 sq in per 1,000 Btu/hr** | 100 sq in each |

The **two-opening outdoor method** requires permanent openings within **12 inches of the ceiling** and within **12 inches of the floor**. For a hotel mechanical room with **two 500 MBH boilers** (1,000,000 Btu/hr total) using direct outdoors or vertical duct: each opening needs **1,000,000 ÷ 4,000 = 250 sq in of free area** — roughly an **18 × 14 inch louver** of free area (gross louver size will be larger after blade obstruction). If the ducts run horizontally for any meaningful distance, the requirement doubles to **1 sq in per 2,000 Btu/hr**, or **500 sq in** each in this example.

The **one-opening method** uses a single opening near the ceiling, sized at **1 sq inch per 3,000 Btu/hr** of total input, with the opening communicating directly with outdoors. The opening also must be at least the cross-sectional area of the largest appliance flue. The appliance needs 1 inch clearance from sides and back, 6 inches from front.

**Key Formula:**  Two-opening (vertical/direct) — each opening = **Total Btu/hr ÷ 4,000 sq in**, min 100 sq in.  Two-opening (horizontal duct) — each opening = **Total Btu/hr ÷ 2,000 sq in**.  One-opening = **Total Btu/hr ÷ 3,000 sq in**.

**Exam Trap:** The most common HVAC exam-day error is applying **1 sq in per 1,000 Btu/hr** to an OUTDOOR scenario. The **1/1,000 rule applies ONLY to the indoor combining-spaces method** (two permanent openings between adjacent rooms, NOT to outdoors — IFGC 304.5.3). For outdoor air, the smallest correct denominator is **3,000 (one opening)** and the most common is **4,000 (two openings, direct/vertical)**.

**Exam Trap:** **Gross area is not free area**. A louvered grille's listed gross area is the rough opening, but its free area (after subtracting blade obstruction) is typically **60–75% for metal louvers, 75% for wood**. If a question gives you a louver size without specifying free area, apply the deduction or you will undersize combustion air.`,

    "Mechanical Combustion Air": `When openings to outdoors are impractical (interior mechanical rooms with no exterior wall, or tight buildings with strict pressurization controls), IFGC 304.6.4 permits **mechanical combustion air supply** — a fan delivering outdoor air directly to the equipment space. The minimum rate is **0.35 cfm per 1,000 Btu/hr** of total input. For a hotel boiler room with **2,000,000 Btu/hr** total input across multiple boilers, the mechanical combustion air fan must deliver **700 cfm** minimum. The fan must be **electrically interlocked** with the appliance burners so that the burners cannot operate if the combustion air fan is not running.

The mechanical combustion air supply must deliver air to the **same space** as the equipment (not into a ductless adjacent room). Where multiple appliances are served, the fan capacity must equal the sum of each appliance's individual requirement, calculated on **total input**. The fan and ductwork must be sized for the calculated airflow with the appliance ductwork closed and only the combustion air fan operating — system effects, louver losses, and duct friction all matter. The mechanical combustion air system must also be permitted to draw air from a source that **cannot become contaminated** during operation (no intake near loading docks, generator exhaust, plumbing vents, or kitchen hood discharge).

**Key Formula:** Mechanical combustion air cfm = **(Total input Btu/hr) × 0.35 ÷ 1,000**

**Exam Trap:** The **electrical interlock** between burner and combustion air fan is a code requirement, not an option. Without it, an inspector will fail the boiler installation even if the fan is correctly sized and operational. The interlock prevents firing without combustion air, which would produce CO and oxygen-deprived combustion in seconds.

**Exam Trap:** **0.35 cfm per 1,000 Btu/hr** is for **mechanical combustion air**. The opening-area rules (1 sq in per 4,000, 3,000, or 2,000 Btu/hr depending on method) are an entirely different unit (sq inches, not cfm). Don't conflate them — a question that gives you fan cfm and asks if it's adequate is testing the 0.35 figure, not the opening-area figures.`
  },

  6: {
    "Vent Types & Clearances": `Venting for fuel-burning appliances is governed by IFGC Chapter 5 (gas) and IMC Chapter 8 (oil, solid fuel, and Category IV special venting). Each vent type has a defined **clearance to combustibles** that must be maintained for the entire run, top to bottom. **Type B vent** (double-wall metal vent for Category I gas appliances) requires **1 inch clearance to combustibles** — or **0 clearance** if the vent is listed and marked for zero clearance. **Single-wall metal vent** requires **6 inches** clearance to combustibles and is generally limited to within the room containing the appliance (it cannot pass through walls, floors, or ceilings to unoccupied spaces). **Masonry chimneys** require **2 inches** clearance to combustibles for interior portions and **1 inch** for exterior portions. **Plastic vent** (PVC/CPVC/polypropylene for Category IV condensing appliances) follows the **appliance manufacturer's specific clearance** as listed — usually 0 inches with proper supports.

Connector pipe (the run between the appliance flue collar and the vertical vent) follows the same clearance as the connector type used. The connector must be **as short and direct as possible**, slope upward toward the vent at a minimum of **1/4 inch per foot**, and the **vent connector horizontal length must not exceed 75% of the height of the vertical vent**. Multiple appliances may share a common vent only when their inputs and vent sizes match a common-vent table (IFGC 504.2 or 504.3) and they are the **same category**.

**Exam Trap:** **Type B vent is 1 inch; single-wall metal is 6 inches.** Students mix these up under time pressure. Remember: Type B is **B**etter insulated → 1 inch. Single-wall has no insulation → 6 inches.

**Exam Trap:** **Single-wall metal vent cannot pass through walls or floors** into unoccupied spaces or concealed framing. It must remain in the room with the appliance unless it transitions to Type B vent at the wall/floor penetration. Many candidates approve a single-wall run that extends into an attic — that's a violation.`,

    "The 3-2-10 Termination Rule": `The chimney and vent termination height rule — universally called the **3-2-10 rule** — is one of the most-tested numbers on F29 and F33. Per IMC 806 / IFGC 503.6.5: a chimney or vent termination must be at least **3 feet above the roof penetration** AND at least **2 feet above any portion of the building structure within 10 feet** measured horizontally. All three numbers (3, 2, 10) must be satisfied simultaneously — the highest of the three controlling heights wins.

Example: a vent penetrating a low roof **8 feet** horizontally from a taller second-story wall must terminate **2 feet above that wall** even though that's more than 3 feet above the roof penetration — because the 2-foot-above rule applies to any portion of the building within 10 feet horizontally. Conversely, a vent on a flat roof with no nearby structure terminates at **3 feet** above the penetration, period. The rule applies to **chimneys, Type B vents, single-wall metal vents, and special vents** alike. It does NOT apply to **direct-vent appliances** with horizontal sidewall terminations — those follow the appliance manufacturer's listed sidewall termination clearances (typically 12 inches above grade, 1 foot from operable openings, etc.).

**Key Formula:** Vent termination height = **MAX (3 ft above roof penetration, 2 ft above any structure within 10 ft horizontal)**.

**Exam Trap:** Students remember "3 feet above the roof" and stop there. The full rule has **three numbers**, and the **2-feet-above-anything-within-10-feet** clause is the one the exam targets. A vent that terminates 3 ft above the roof but only 1 ft above an adjacent parapet wall fails the rule.

**Exam Trap:** The 3-2-10 rule **does not apply to direct-vent appliances** that terminate through a sidewall. Direct-vent sidewall terminations follow a separate set of clearance rules (typically from manufacturer's instructions and IFGC 503.8). Applying 3-2-10 to a sidewall vent installation is a common wrong-answer trap.`,

    "Vent & Connector Sizing Tables": `Vent and vent connector sizing is performed by lookup in **IFGC Tables 504.2 (single-appliance Type B)** and **504.3 (multiple-appliance common vent)**. The variables are: appliance input in **thousands of Btu/hr**, vent height in **feet**, lateral connector length in feet, and the number of elbows in the connector. The table outputs the minimum vent diameter that will produce adequate draft. For a single **150,000 Btu/hr** natural-draft furnace with a **20-foot vertical Type B vent** and a **5-foot lateral connector with two 90° elbows**, you'd consult Table 504.2 and find a **4-inch diameter** typically suffices.

For multiple appliances on a **common vent**, you use Table 504.3, which has two columns: the **maximum combined input for the common vent** (based on height and the smallest vent connector size) and the **maximum input for each connector**. Common venting only works when **all appliances are the same vent category** (typically all Category I) and the **smaller appliance's connector enters the common vent above the larger appliance's connector** to prevent backdrafting the smaller appliance. The **connector horizontal length cannot exceed 75% of the vertical vent height** — exceed this and the table is invalid and the system will not draft properly.

**Exam Trap:** The **75% rule** for connector length is absolute. A **15-foot vertical vent** allows a maximum **11.25-foot horizontal connector run** (15 × 0.75). Students who use longer connectors on short vents will see backdrafting and CO problems in the field — and miss this question on the exam.

**Exam Trap:** **You cannot common-vent a Category I and a Category IV appliance.** The pressure differential will backdraft the negative-pressure Category I appliance. When a high-efficiency Category IV furnace is added to a home that previously had a common Type B vent for a Category I water heater, the Cat I water heater must be re-vented separately (or replaced with a power-vent or direct-vent unit).`
  },

  7: {
    "Furnaces, Boilers, Unit Heaters": `IMC Chapter 9 and IFGC Chapter 6 cover specific appliances. **Furnaces** (forced-air heating appliances) must be installed with the **manufacturer's listed clearances** to combustibles — typically **6 inches** on sides and back, **24 inches** at the front for service, and **1 inch** at the top for a single-wall flue (or 0 with Type B). A furnace closet door must have clearance equal to the equipment's required service space when the door is open. **Furnaces installed in a garage** must have the burner or ignition source **at least 18 inches above the floor** (IMC 304.3) — unless the furnace is direct-vent or listed FVIR.

**Boilers** per IMC Chapter 10 require ASME stamping for pressure vessels, a **low water cutoff** on all automatically fired steam and hot water boilers, and a **pressure relief valve** discharging to a safe location with **no valves of any kind** between the boiler and the relief valve. Boiler service clearance defaults to **24 inches** if the manufacturer doesn't specify more. **Unit heaters** (gas-fired, suspended overhead) require clearances per the manufacturer's listing — clearances to combustibles are typically **6 inches** above and to the sides, with **no clearance required below** for most listed units. The **18-inch dimension often quoted** for unit heaters is the **service/working clearance below the unit** (for filter changes, burner access, etc.) — it is NOT a clearance-to-combustibles. Unit heaters must be **supported independently** of the gas piping and vent connector — neither the gas line nor the vent may be used as structural support.

**Exam Trap:** The **no-valve-on-relief-line rule** applies to **between the boiler and the relief valve**. A shutoff downstream of the relief discharge is acceptable for testing/maintenance, but anything that could isolate the relief from the boiler is prohibited. Students miss this because the rule is so absolute it sounds like a trick question.

**Exam Trap:** A **gas unit heater hung from a gas line** is a common field error — the gas piping is **not a structural support** and using it as one violates IFGC 408. Unit heaters must be supported by independent chain, threaded rod, or strap attached to the building structure.`,

    "Unvented Room Heaters": `Unvented (vent-free) gas room heaters per IFGC 621 burn all combustion products into the room they heat — there is no flue. They are permitted in most jurisdictions but heavily restricted as to size, location, and oxygen-depletion safety. Maximum input rating is **40,000 Btu/hr** for vent-free room heaters in any single room, and **10,000 Btu/hr** in bedrooms or bathrooms. Each unit must be equipped with an **Oxygen Depletion Sensor (ODS)** pilot that shuts off the gas if room oxygen falls below **18%** (normal atmospheric oxygen is 20.9%). The room must have at least **50 cubic feet per 1,000 Btu/hr** of unit input — same volume rule as the indoor air combustion-air method.

Unvented room heaters are **prohibited** in: bathrooms over 6,000 Btu/hr, bedrooms over 10,000 Btu/hr, multifamily dwelling unit common areas, and any unconfined or below-grade space where combustion products could accumulate. Several states (California, Colorado in some jurisdictions, and Massachusetts) restrict or prohibit them entirely — verify Denver-specific amendments before installing. The exhaust products (water vapor and CO2 primarily, with trace CO) raise indoor humidity and can cause condensation, mold, and indoor air quality complaints even when the unit is operating correctly.

**Exam Trap:** **Bedroom and bathroom limits are absolute and small**: **10,000 Btu/hr in bedrooms**, **6,000 Btu/hr in bathrooms**. Many installers assume the 40,000 Btu/hr general limit applies everywhere — it does not. A 20,000 Btu/hr vent-free heater in a master bedroom is a code violation regardless of room volume.

**Exam Trap:** The **ODS shuts off at 18% oxygen**, not at a CO threshold. Vent-free heaters don't have built-in CO detectors — they rely on the oxygen sensor as a proxy. If a question asks what the ODS measures, the answer is **oxygen concentration**, not carbon monoxide.`,

    "Fireplaces & Decorative Appliances": `Decorative gas appliances (vented gas logs, B-vent gas fireplaces, direct-vent gas fireplaces) per IFGC 602–605 are categorized by their venting and combustion air configuration. **Vented gas logs in a solid-fuel fireplace** require the **damper to be permanently fixed in the open position** (or fully removed) to prevent CO accumulation if the homeowner closes it. **B-vent gas fireplaces** vent through Type B vent with **1-inch clearance to combustibles** and draw combustion air from the room. **Direct-vent gas fireplaces** are **sealed-combustion** units: they draw combustion air from outdoors through a coaxial or two-pipe vent and exhaust products outdoors through the same penetration — they do not affect indoor air at all and can be installed in bedrooms.

**Decorative appliances installed in solid-fuel fireplaces** must be listed for that application and the chimney must be in working condition. The minimum chimney height for a masonry fireplace is **15 feet** measured from the firebox floor (this is a fireplace structural requirement, not a vent-termination rule — vent termination still follows 3-2-10). Direct-vent terminations follow the manufacturer's listed clearances: typically **12 inches above grade**, **12 inches from operable windows or doors**, **3 feet from forced-air intakes**, and **4 feet horizontally below or beside any opening**.

**Exam Trap:** A **damper clamp keeping the damper open** is required when vented gas logs are installed in a previously wood-burning fireplace. If the homeowner can close the damper, CO from the gas logs will spill into the room. The clamp is a permanent device (not a sticker) and is a hard code requirement.

**Exam Trap:** **Direct-vent fireplaces can be installed in bedrooms** because they are sealed-combustion. Vented gas logs, B-vent gas fireplaces, and unvented gas fireplaces cannot. The exam will sometimes describe an installation in a bedroom and ask which appliance type is permitted — the answer is direct-vent.`
  },

  8: {
    "Outdoor Air Rates (Table 403.3.1.1)": `IMC 403 and Table 403.3.1.1 set the minimum outdoor air ventilation rates for commercial occupancies. The table gives **two numbers per occupancy**: a **people component** (cfm per person) and an **area component** (cfm per square foot). You must calculate both and **add them** — the total is the minimum required outdoor air. **Hotel guest rooms** are calculated at **5 cfm/person + 0.06 cfm/sq ft**. A 300 sq ft guest room designed for 2 occupants requires **10 cfm (people) + 18 cfm (area) = 28 cfm** of outdoor air. **Hotel lobbies and conference rooms** use higher rates — lobbies at **7.5 cfm/person + 0.06 cfm/sq ft**, conference at **5 cfm/person + 0.06 cfm/sq ft** at a design density of 50 people per 1,000 sq ft.

**Restaurant dining** is **7.5 cfm/person + 0.18 cfm/sq ft**; **office space** is **5 cfm/person + 0.06 cfm/sq ft**; **classrooms (ages 5-8)** are **10 cfm/person + 0.12 cfm/sq ft**; **patient rooms** are **25 cfm/person**; **gymnasiums** are **20 cfm/person**. **Smoking lounges** (where permitted) jump to **60 cfm/person**. **Locker rooms** are calculated by **0.5 cfm/sq ft** only (no people component). Outdoor air must be delivered through the supply system and may be reduced by air cleaning credits (IAQ procedure) per ASHRAE 62.1 — but the **default ventilation rate procedure** is what's tested on F29.

**Key Formula:** Required OA cfm = **(occupant count × cfm/person) + (sq ft × cfm/sq ft)**

**Exam Trap:** **Both components must be added**. A hotel guest room of 350 sq ft with 2 design occupants requires **10 + 21 = 31 cfm**, not just 10. Students who use only the per-person number for "people-driven" spaces consistently undersize ventilation.

**Exam Trap:** **Design occupant load** is what drives the per-person calculation, not actual occupancy. For a 200-seat banquet hall, you use **200 people** regardless of how many actually show up. Default occupancy density from Table 403.3.1.1 (e.g., **70 people per 1,000 sq ft** for restaurant dining) applies when occupancy is unknown or not yet defined.`,

    "Bathroom & Kitchen Local Exhaust": `Local exhaust requirements in IMC Table 403.3.1.1 supplement the general outdoor air rates. **Bathrooms (private, residential)** require **50 cfm intermittent OR 20 cfm continuous** — both options are code-compliant. **Public restrooms in commercial buildings** require **50 cfm/water closet or urinal** (whichever is greater) of dedicated exhaust. **Residential kitchens** require **100 cfm intermittent OR 25 cfm continuous** through a range hood. **Commercial kitchens** are governed separately under IMC Chapter 5 and require Type I or Type II hoods sized by hood length and cooking surface — not a flat cfm number.

Bathroom exhaust must terminate outdoors, **not into an attic or crawl space**. Termination must be at least **3 feet** from openings into the building and from property lines. Kitchen exhaust (residential) follows the same outdoor termination requirement and must not be ducted into another exhaust system. **Hotel guest room bathrooms** typically run continuous exhaust at 20 cfm because intermittent operation is inconvenient and unreliable in transient occupancy. Bathrooms with no openable window per IMC 401.2 **must** have mechanical exhaust — bathrooms with an openable window of adequate size may use natural ventilation in lieu of mechanical (rare in modern construction).

**Exam Trap:** **Bathroom exhaust may be intermittent OR continuous**, but the cfm values are different. **50 cfm intermittent**, **20 cfm continuous**. Students assume the rate is always 50 cfm regardless of operation mode. A 100 cfm continuous fan is overkill; a 20 cfm intermittent fan is undersized.

**Exam Trap:** **Bathroom exhaust ducted into the attic is a violation** in every model code — and a real-world source of mold and rot. The exhaust must extend through the roof or sidewall to outdoors. A common wrong answer suggests terminating "in a soffit-vented attic" — that's still a code violation.`,

    "Outdoor Intake Separation": `IMC 401.4 and Table 401.5 govern minimum separation distances between outdoor air intakes and potential sources of contamination. The general rule is **10 feet minimum** horizontal separation from: **plumbing vent terminations**, **combustion exhaust outlets**, **dryer exhaust**, **loading docks**, **driveways** with parked vehicles, **trash storage areas**, **cooling tower discharge**, and **streets**. The separation may be reduced to **3 feet below** a plumbing vent. **Hazardous exhaust outlets** (Type I commercial kitchen hood, fume hoods, refrigerant relief discharge) require **30 feet** of separation from any outdoor air intake.

Outdoor air intakes must also be located at least **10 feet above grade** when located adjacent to a street to avoid pulling vehicle exhaust into the building. Intakes must be screened or louvered to prevent entry of birds, rodents, leaves, and snow. Snow load and snow buildup against an intake louver is a Denver-specific consideration — intake bottoms should be at least **18 inches above the maximum anticipated snow depth** to prevent blockage in winter. Intakes serving a refrigeration machinery room or other space with potential refrigerant release must be located **above any potential refrigerant accumulation zone** (refrigerants heavier than air settle low; intakes high prevent recirculation).

**Exam Trap:** **30 feet** is the separation for **hazardous exhaust** — Type I kitchen hood discharge, refrigerant relief, fume hood exhaust. **10 feet** is the separation for **general contaminants** — plumbing vents, dryers, driveways. Students remember "10 feet" for everything and miss the hazardous-exhaust questions. Memorize: **hazardous = 30**, **general = 10**.

**Exam Trap:** **Plumbing vent separation** is **10 feet horizontal or 3 feet below**. The "or 3 feet below" alternative is testable — an intake located **3 feet below a plumbing vent** is code-compliant even if it's less than 10 feet horizontally separated, because the rising vent gases carry contaminants upward.`
  },

  9: {
    "Type I & Type II Commercial Kitchen Hoods": `IMC Chapter 5 and NFPA 96 govern commercial kitchen exhaust hoods. **Type I hoods** are required over cooking appliances that produce **grease-laden vapors** — fryers, griddles, charbroilers, ranges, woks, and similar. **Type II hoods** are for appliances that produce only **heat, steam, or moisture** — dishwashers, steamers, ovens, and water-bath warmers. Type I hoods are heavier-gauge stainless or galvanized steel with welded liquid-tight joints, integrated grease filters or baffles, and a continuous **slope of 2% (1/4 inch per foot)** in the hood plenum toward a **grease reservoir or drain**. **Cleanouts are required every 20 feet** in horizontal Type I duct runs and at every change of direction.

Type I hood overhang must extend **at least 6 inches beyond the cooking surface** on all open sides. Hood clearance to combustibles is **18 inches** unless field-applied protection (per NFPA 96) reduces it. Type I exhaust duct material is **minimum 16-gauge carbon steel** (or **18-gauge stainless**) with all joints **continuously welded liquid-tight**. The exhaust duct must be enclosed in a **fire-rated shaft** when passing through multiple floors (rating per IBC). Make-up air per IMC 508 must be supplied at **a rate that prevents negative pressure exceeding 0.02 inches w.c. relative to outdoors**, and is typically introduced as **tempered (heated/cooled) outside air** through a dedicated make-up air unit.

**Exam Trap:** The **6-inch overhang** applies to **open sides only**. A hood mounted against a wall does not need 6 inches of overhang on the wall side because there is no open side there. Don't apply the overhang rule to enclosed sides.

**Exam Trap:** **Type I duct must be continuously welded liquid-tight.** Mechanical seams, screwed connections, or sealant-only joints are violations even when properly installed. Liquid-tight means **welded by a qualified welder** — and the welds are subject to inspection.`,

    "Dryer Exhaust 35-Foot Rule": `Domestic clothes dryer exhaust per IMC 504.6.4 has strict length limits because lint buildup is a major fire hazard. The maximum total developed length of dryer exhaust is **35 feet**, measured from the dryer connection to the outdoor termination. Deductions apply for fittings: **subtract 5 feet for each 90° elbow** and **2.5 feet for each 45° elbow**. A run with three 90° elbows already loses **15 feet** before the straight pipe is counted — leaving only **20 feet of straight run** before the 35-foot maximum is reached. The dryer **manufacturer's specifications override** if they are more restrictive (some high-efficiency dryers allow only 25 feet maximum).

Dryer exhaust duct must be **rigid metal**, **minimum 4 inches** in diameter, with **smooth interior walls** to minimize lint accumulation. **No screws or fasteners may protrude into the interior** of the duct — use crimped joints with foil tape, no sheet-metal screws. The duct must terminate **outdoors with a backdraft damper** and must **not terminate into a chimney, vent, crawl space, or attic**. Outdoor termination must be at least **3 feet from any opening into the building**. Flexible transition duct (between the dryer and the wall connection) is permitted only as a listed transition duct (UL 2158A) and is limited to **6 feet** in length.

**Key Formula:** Allowable Straight Run = **35 ft − (5 ft × number of 90° elbows) − (2.5 ft × number of 45° elbows)** [or manufacturer's limit if shorter]

**Exam Trap:** The **35 feet is total developed length, NOT straight run**. A common wrong answer says "you can have 35 feet of straight duct plus a few elbows." The reality is that **each 90° elbow eats 5 feet** of your allowance.

**Exam Trap:** **Screws inside the duct are prohibited** because they catch lint and create fire hazards. Many installers use sheet-metal screws to assemble dryer ducting — that's a code violation even though the duct otherwise looks correct. Use crimped joints, foil tape, or listed UL 181 mastic.`,

    "Hazardous Exhaust & Parking Garages": `Hazardous exhaust systems per IMC 510 handle air contaminated with flammable vapors, fumes, dust, or smoke from hazardous processes. Hazardous exhaust must be **dedicated** — not combined with general exhaust or return air. The duct must be **non-combustible**, **gas-tight**, and constructed of material compatible with the contaminant (stainless steel for corrosive vapors, FRP for some chemical fumes). Hazardous exhaust **terminations must be at least 30 feet from any outdoor air intake** (Table 401.5). Discharge must be vertical and unobstructed by rain caps that would deflect the discharge horizontally.

**Parking garage ventilation** per IMC 404 requires CO and NO2 control. Enclosed parking garages must have mechanical ventilation at **0.75 cfm per square foot** of floor area, OR a **CO/NO2 demand-controlled system** that ramps ventilation up when sensors detect elevated levels. The demand-controlled approach is preferred for energy efficiency but requires **CO sensors** at a rate of **at least one per 5,000 sq ft** and at a **maximum spacing of 100 feet** between sensors. The system must alarm at **CO levels above 25 ppm time-weighted average** and shut down vehicles or ramp ventilation to full at higher levels. Repair garages have additional requirements per IMC 502.14 because of vehicle exhaust during indoor running and refrigerant work.

**Exam Trap:** **30 feet** separation for hazardous exhaust, **10 feet** for general outdoor air contaminants. A laboratory fume hood (hazardous) ducted to a discharge **15 feet from an intake** is a violation even though it would pass general contaminant separation.

**Exam Trap:** Parking garage **demand-controlled ventilation** is allowed but requires sensors and controls per IMC 404. Going to demand-controlled does not relieve you of the requirement — it changes how you meet it. Many candidates assume demand-controlled means "no required minimum airflow" — wrong. Minimum airflow is still required; the demand controls just modulate it.`
  },

  10: {
    "Duct Materials & Insulation": `IMC Chapter 6 specifies acceptable duct materials and minimum insulation. Approved metal duct materials include **galvanized steel**, **aluminum**, **stainless steel**, and **copper**. Non-metal materials include **factory-made air duct (UL 181)**, **rigid fibrous glass duct board** (with proper closure systems), and **gypsum board ducts** (only where specifically permitted by code). PVC ducting is generally permitted only in **specific applications** such as condensate drainage and certain corrosive exhaust — not for general HVAC supply or return. **Underground ducts** must be **encased in at least 2 inches of concrete** or constructed of approved corrosion-resistant material, sealed against moisture intrusion, and pitched to drain to a low-point sump.

Duct insulation R-values are set by the IECC, not the IMC, and depend on **climate zone and duct location**. Denver is in **climate zone 5B**, which generally requires **R-6 minimum on supply ducts and R-3 on return ducts in unconditioned spaces**, climbing to **R-8 or higher** for ducts in attics or outdoors. Ducts inside the conditioned space (within the building thermal envelope) typically have no insulation requirement but still benefit from sealing for acoustics and energy. Duct sealing per IECC must meet specific leakage thresholds verified by a duct leakage test in residential new construction.

**Exam Trap:** **Underground duct must be encased in 2 inches of concrete** OR be a listed corrosion-resistant material. Burying galvanized steel duct directly in soil is a violation that fails fast — galvanized corrodes within years underground, and water infiltration follows.

**Exam Trap:** The R-value requirement is based on **duct location** (conditioned vs unconditioned space), not just on whether the duct is "indoors." A supply duct running through an **unconditioned attic** in Denver needs **R-8** even though the attic is technically inside the roof. Conditioned space means inside the insulated envelope, not just inside the roof.`,

    "Plenums & Return Air Restrictions": `A plenum per IMC 202 is a compartment to which one or more ducts connect, that forms part of the air distribution system, and that is not used for housing personnel. Common examples: the space above a suspended ceiling used to return air, an air handler's mixing box, a furnace cabinet. **Combustible materials in plenums** are restricted by IMC 602.2 — only materials with a **flame spread index of 25 or less and smoke-developed index of 50 or less** are permitted unless the material is enclosed in a metal raceway or has other approved protection. **Plenum-rated cable** (CMP for communications, MPP for power-limited) is required for any cable run in a return-air plenum.

Return air per IMC 601.5 must NOT be drawn from: **closets** (other than laundry closets containing only laundry equipment), **kitchens**, **bathrooms**, **toilet rooms**, **garages**, **mechanical rooms** containing fuel-burning equipment with atmospheric burners, **refrigeration machinery rooms**, **storage rooms for hazardous materials**, or **rooms containing fuel-fired equipment with combustion air drawn from the same space**. Combustion air for fuel-burning appliances must **never** share the return-air path. A common code violation is a return grille in a garage that pulls CO and VOCs into the living space — even a small leak in a ducted return that passes through the garage will draw contaminated air.

**Exam Trap:** **Combustion air must not combine with return air**. A high-efficiency furnace with a sealed combustion intake from outdoors is fine, but a furnace drawing combustion air from the same room as a return-air grille creates a hazard. Inspectors test this with a smoke pencil — if the smoke gets pulled into the return, the installation fails.

**Exam Trap:** A **laundry closet** can have return air through its door undercut or grille — laundry is the **exception** to the closet prohibition. Bedrooms typically can have returns (some jurisdictions require a return per bedroom for pressurization). The prohibition is targeted at closets that store unrelated items, garages, bathrooms, kitchens, and mechanical rooms.`,

    "Underground & Flexible Duct": `**Underground duct** per IMC 603.8 must be **encased in at least 2 inches of concrete** or constructed of an approved **corrosion-resistant material** suitable for the soil conditions. Plastic ducts (PVC, ABS) listed for underground HVAC use are permitted with appropriate joining methods. The duct must be sloped to **drain to a low-point sump or condensate drain** because groundwater and condensation will collect. Underground ducts servicing the supply side carry conditioned air to floor registers in slab-on-grade construction.

**Flexible duct** per IMC 603.6 and UL 181 is permitted with limitations. **Listed flexible duct (Class 0 or Class 1)** may be used for supply and return duct connections, but **the maximum length is 5 feet** when used as a connector between rigid ductwork and a terminal device — longer runs are permitted where the flexible duct is the listed primary duct, but most jurisdictions limit it to 14 feet per branch and prohibit it from passing through walls, floors, or ceilings. Flexible duct must be **supported at least every 4 feet** with at least **1-1/2 inch wide saddles or hangers** to prevent kinking. Sharp bends (radius less than the duct diameter) substantially reduce airflow and are an installation defect even when "code-compliant" on length.

**Exam Trap:** **Flexible duct cannot pass through walls, floors, or ceilings** in most jurisdictions. The installer who runs flex through a fire-rated assembly defeats both the duct's listing and the fire rating. Use rigid duct through penetrations and transition to flex at the terminal.

**Exam Trap:** Flexible duct **must be supported every 4 feet** to prevent sag and kinking. A sagging flex duct can lose **50% or more** of its design airflow due to obstruction at the sag point. Inspectors look for proper hanger spacing — exam questions test the **4-foot** number.`
  },

  11: {
    "Low Water Cutoff & Relief Valves": `IMC Chapter 10 governs boilers and pressure vessels. **Low water cutoff (LWCO)** is mandatory on **all automatically fired steam and hot water boilers** per IMC 1006. The LWCO senses water level and shuts off the burner when water drops below a safe operating level — preventing the boiler from firing dry, which would crack the heat exchanger and potentially cause an explosion. Steam boilers require **two LWCOs** in series (primary plus secondary/backup) per most jurisdictions. Hot water boilers typically require one LWCO unless serving a critical application. The LWCO must be **tested per the manufacturer's schedule** — typically monthly during the heating season — and a test log is required by some AHJs.

**Pressure relief valves (PRVs)** per IMC 1006.4 must be installed on every boiler and discharge to a **safe location** — typically outdoors or to a floor drain that can handle the discharge temperature and flow. **No valves of any kind may be installed between the boiler and the relief valve** — not a service valve, not a ball valve, not even a check valve. The relief valve must be sized to discharge the boiler's full input capacity at the relief setpoint without exceeding the design pressure of the vessel by more than 10%. PRV setpoint must be **at or below the maximum allowable working pressure (MAWP)** of the boiler (typically 30 psi for residential hot water, higher for steam and commercial).

**Exam Trap:** **No valve of any kind between the boiler and relief valve.** This rule is so absolute that even a "tagged-open" ball valve for testing is a violation. The exam will offer a wrong answer like "a ball valve permanently tagged open is acceptable" — it is not.

**Exam Trap:** **Steam boilers require two LWCOs** (primary and secondary) on most commercial installations. Hot water boilers typically require one. Students who memorize "one LWCO per boiler" miss the steam-boiler double-requirement question.`,

    "Boiler Room Requirements": `A boiler room must provide adequate **combustion air per IFGC 304**, adequate **clearance for service** (typically **24 inches at the front** unless the boiler manufacturer specifies more), and protection of structural components from heat. **Floor protection** under solid-fuel and oil-fired boilers is required — typically a non-combustible pad extending **18 inches beyond the boiler footprint** on all sides where embers or fuel spills could occur. **Boiler room ventilation** must serve double duty: provide combustion air AND prevent the room from overheating. The IMC defers to IFGC 304 for combustion air calculations and to manufacturer's installation instructions for any equipment-specific ventilation rates.

**Emergency shutoffs** are required for boiler installations. A **remote emergency shutoff switch** must be located at the **principal entry to the boiler room** — outside the room or at the doorway — so a worker can shut down the system without entering an unsafe area. The switch must be labeled "EMERGENCY BOILER SHUTOFF" or similar and color-coded **red**. **Gas piping** to the boiler must have a shutoff valve **within 6 feet of the appliance** and a **sediment trap (drip leg)** immediately upstream of the appliance gas connection (sediment trap is required for boilers — unlike ranges and dryers, which are exempt).

**Exam Trap:** **Sediment trap (drip leg) IS required for boilers** — they are not in the exception list for IFGC 408.4. The exception list (no drip leg required) covers **ranges, illuminating appliances, clothes dryers, outdoor grills, and gas lights**. Boilers, furnaces, and water heaters all require sediment traps.

**Exam Trap:** The **emergency shutoff must be OUTSIDE the boiler room** at the principal entry — not inside the room. The whole point is to allow shutdown without entering a potentially dangerous area. An inside switch is a violation regardless of how easily accessible it is.`,

    "ASME Pressure Vessel Stamps": `Per IMC 1004, all **boilers and pressure vessels** constructed for use within the IMC must bear the **ASME (American Society of Mechanical Engineers) Code stamp** appropriate to their service. For boilers: **Section I** (Power Boilers) for high-pressure steam, **Section IV** (Heating Boilers) for low-pressure heating boilers (15 psig steam or less, 160 psig water at 250°F or less). The **"S" stamp** designates Section I; the **"H" stamp** designates Section IV heating boilers. Pressure vessels other than boilers are stamped per **Section VIII** ("U" stamp). The stamp certifies that the vessel was designed, fabricated, and tested in accordance with ASME requirements and has been inspected by an authorized inspector.

The boiler's **manufacturer's data report** (Form U-1 for vessels, Form H-3 for heating boilers) must be available to the AHJ. The data plate, attached to the boiler, shows the **maximum allowable working pressure (MAWP)**, the **maximum temperature**, the **heating surface area**, and the **National Board registration number** (if the boiler is registered with the National Board of Boiler and Pressure Vessel Inspectors). Denver and most Colorado jurisdictions require boilers to be **registered with the state boiler inspector** and inspected annually for hot water boilers above a threshold and biennially for low-pressure steam — verify with Denver CPD and the Colorado Division of Oil and Public Safety boiler section.

**Exam Trap:** The **"H" stamp is for heating boilers** (Section IV); the **"S" stamp is for power boilers** (Section I). They are not interchangeable. A heating boiler stamped "S" was constructed to higher (power boiler) standards and is still acceptable; a power boiler stamped only "H" is **not** acceptable for high-pressure steam service.

**Exam Trap:** **Pressure vessels other than boilers** (expansion tanks, hot water storage tanks, air receivers) bear the **"U" stamp** under Section VIII. An exam question that lists a pressurized expansion tank and asks which stamp applies is testing Section VIII, not Section IV.`
  },

  12: {
    "Machinery Room Requirements": `IMC Chapter 11 (cross-referencing ASHRAE 15) governs refrigeration machinery rooms. A **machinery room** is required when **refrigerant quantity exceeds the quantity-per-occupancy thresholds** in IMC Table 1103.1 for the specific refrigerant class and the building occupancy class. Once a machinery room is required, the room must be **separated from the rest of the building by construction of at least 1-hour fire-resistance rating**, must have **no openings into other occupied spaces** other than tight-fitting access doors with self-closers, and must have **dedicated ventilation** that does not communicate with any other ventilation system.

Machinery room **ventilation** per IMC 1105 has two modes. **Continuous ventilation** at **0.5 cfm per square foot** of room floor area (minimum) for normal operation. **Emergency mechanical ventilation** activated by the refrigerant detector at the **calculated emergency rate** per ASHRAE 15, which is sized to **maintain refrigerant concentration below the IDLH (Immediately Dangerous to Life or Health) limit**. The emergency rate is often expressed as **30 cfm per person** OR a calculated value based on refrigerant quantity — whichever is greater. **Emergency ventilation fans must be on emergency power** if the building has a generator, and must vent to **outdoors at least 20 feet from any building opening** for high-density refrigerants.

**Exam Trap:** Machinery rooms have **two ventilation rates**: continuous baseline AND emergency on detection. Students remember one and forget the other. The emergency rate is sized to clear the room to safe concentration in the event of a refrigerant release; the continuous rate is a baseline.

**Exam Trap:** Machinery rooms must have **tight-fitting self-closing doors** — never propped open. Propping a door for "ventilation" defeats the purpose of the room (containing a release) and is a violation even if the room would otherwise be in compliance.`,

    "Refrigerant Classifications (A1, A2L, B1)": `ASHRAE 34 classifies refrigerants by **toxicity (Class A or B)** and **flammability (Class 1, 2L, 2, or 3)**. Class A = lower toxicity; Class B = higher toxicity. Class 1 = no flame propagation; Class 2L = lower flammability with burn velocity ≤10 cm/s; Class 2 = lower flammability; Class 3 = higher flammability. Combined classifications: **A1** (lowest hazard — R-410A, R-134a, R-22, R-404A, R-744 CO2), **A2L** (mildly flammable — R-32, R-454B, R-1234yf), **A3** (high flammability — R-290 propane), **B1** (toxic but non-flammable — R-123 in older centrifugal chillers, R-717 ammonia is B2L not B1), **B2L/B2** (mildly toxic, mildly flammable — ammonia).

In a Denver hotel kitchen with **A1 commercial refrigeration** (R-404A walk-ins, R-410A rooftop A/C), refrigerant quantities under occupancy thresholds may not trigger a machinery room. In a hotel chiller plant with a **B1 centrifugal chiller** (R-123) or any **A2L refrigerant** system over the threshold, a machinery room IS required with all associated ventilation, detection, and emergency-switch requirements. **A2L refrigerants** (R-32, R-454B) require **enhanced ventilation, ignition source restrictions, and refrigerant detection** even in residential applications — the 2018 IMC and 2024 IMC added specific A2L provisions.

**Exam Trap:** **R-410A is A1, R-32 is A2L** — even though R-32 is a component of R-410A. The blend's flammability is dominated by the non-flammable components; the single-component R-32 retains its A2L classification. Don't assume blend properties from constituent properties.

**Exam Trap:** **A2L is not the same as A3**. A2L is "mildly flammable" (low burn velocity, low heat of combustion) — restricted but allowed in many residential and commercial applications. A3 (propane R-290) is fully flammable and restricted to small factory-sealed units. Mixing these up will produce wrong answers on quantity-limit and machinery-room questions.`,

    "Emergency Switches & Detectors": `Two critical safety devices are required in any refrigeration machinery room. The **emergency control switch** per IMC 1105.6.2 must be **located outside the machinery room** at the **principal entry door** — never inside. The switch shuts down all refrigeration equipment, activates emergency ventilation at maximum, and energizes alarm systems. The switch must be **clearly identified as the emergency refrigeration shutoff** and protected from accidental operation (typically a guarded or recessed pushbutton). A second switch is permitted inside the room but only as an additional means — the outside-the-room switch is **mandatory**.

**Refrigerant detectors** per IMC 1105.3 must be installed in machinery rooms with all refrigerants. The detector must be located at the **point of highest expected refrigerant concentration** based on refrigerant density: **heavier-than-air refrigerants** (most HFCs, including R-410A, R-134a, R-404A) require a detector **near the floor**; **lighter-than-air refrigerants** (ammonia R-717) require a detector **near the ceiling**. Detector alarm setpoints must be **at or below 25% of the refrigerant's IDLH** concentration. The detector must activate the **emergency ventilation, alarm at the building fire alarm panel, and (in some jurisdictions) shut off the refrigeration compressors automatically**.

**Exam Trap:** The **emergency switch must be OUTSIDE the machinery room** at the principal entrance — installing it inside the room is a code violation because in an emergency a worker should not have to enter a refrigerant-contaminated room to shut down the equipment. This is one of the top 12 HVAC exam traps.

**Exam Trap:** **Heavier-than-air refrigerants** (R-410A, R-22, R-134a, R-404A) settle low — put the detector **near the floor**. **Lighter-than-air refrigerants** (ammonia, hydrogen) rise — put the detector **near the ceiling**. A detector at the wrong elevation will fail to alarm during a real release.`
  },

  13: {
    "Expansion Tanks & Air Separators": `IMC Chapter 12 governs hydronic piping for closed-loop hot water and chilled water systems. An **expansion tank** is required on every closed hydronic system to absorb the volume change of water as it heats (water expands roughly **4% from 50°F to 200°F**). Two main types: **diaphragm/bladder tanks** (pre-charged with air on one side, water on the other, separated by a flexible membrane) and **plain steel compression tanks** (older technology, requires manual air management). Diaphragm tanks are sized based on **system volume**, **temperature range**, and **system pressure** — typical sizing formula uses ASHRAE methods or manufacturer software.

**Air separators** remove dissolved and entrained air from the circulating water. Air in a hydronic system causes **noise, pump cavitation, corrosion, and reduced heat transfer**. The air separator is typically installed **on the supply side of the boiler at the point of highest temperature and lowest pressure** (where dissolved air is most likely to come out of solution) and works in conjunction with an **automatic air vent** that releases collected air to atmosphere. Many modern systems use an **air-and-dirt separator** combined into a single unit that also drops out particulates. The system must include **fill valves** (typically a pressure-reducing valve set to maintain minimum system pressure) and **manual or automatic makeup water** to compensate for small leaks.

**Exam Trap:** Expansion tanks are **mandatory** on closed hydronic systems — the system **will fail** without one. As water heats, it must go somewhere; without an expansion tank, system pressure rises until the relief valve lifts (wasting water and air-binding the system). Open systems (gravity feed) don't need expansion tanks; closed systems always do.

**Exam Trap:** The air separator goes **on the supply side at the boiler outlet** — the point of highest temperature and lowest pressure in the loop. Installing it on the return side or far from the boiler reduces its effectiveness because air is more dissolved in cooler, higher-pressure water and won't separate out.`,

    "Pipe Insulation & R-values": `Hydronic pipe insulation is required by the IECC (referenced by IMC 1204) for **piping conveying fluids hotter than 105°F or cooler than 55°F**. Thickness is set by **IECC Table C403.11.3** based on **operating temperature range, pipe nominal size, and insulation conductivity** (assumed 0.27 Btu·in/(hr·ft²·°F) at 75°F mean). Memorize the structure of the table:

| Fluid temp | Pipe ≤1" | 1.25"–2" | 2.5"–4" | 5"–6" | >8" |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| **>350°F (HP steam)** | 4.5" | 5.0" | 5.0" | 5.0" | 5.0" |
| **251–350°F** | 3.0" | 4.0" | 4.5" | 4.5" | 4.5" |
| **201–250°F** | 2.5" | 2.5" | 2.5" | 3.0" | 3.0" |
| **141–200°F** | 1.5" | 1.5" | 2.0" | 2.0" | 2.0" |
| **105–140°F (hot water)** | 1.0" | 1.0" | 1.5" | 1.5" | 1.5" |
| **40–60°F (chilled, brine)** | 0.5" | 0.5" | 1.0" | 1.0" | 1.0" |
| **<40°F (refrig)** | 0.5" | 1.0" | 1.0" | 1.5" | 1.5" |

Materials with lower conductivity (closed-cell elastomeric ≈0.25, polyurethane foam ≈0.16) may be installed thinner; higher-conductivity materials (cellular glass ≈0.40) must be thicker. **Equivalent R-value** is thickness ÷ conductivity — a 1.5-inch fiberglass at 0.27 conductivity is approximately **R-5.5**. Insulation in plenums or occupied spaces must also meet **flame spread ≤25 / smoke-developed ≤50** per IMC 604.3 (this is a separate fire requirement, not the thermal R-value).

**Pipe support spacing** per MSS SP-58 and IMC 305 depends on pipe material and size. **Steel pipe 1-inch diameter** supports every **7 feet maximum**; **steel pipe 4-inch diameter** every **14 feet**. **Copper pipe** is more flexible and is supported more frequently — 1-inch copper at **6 feet maximum** typically. **PEX and CPVC** require closer support than metal because they sag under temperature — typical PEX support every **32 inches horizontal**, vertical at every floor. Riser clamps are required at each floor for vertical piping. Insulation thickness affects support type — saddles and shields prevent the insulation from being crushed by hangers.

**Exam Trap:** **Insulation flame spread/smoke-developed** ratings of **25/50** are required in plenums and occupied spaces. Common low-cost fiberglass without a fire-rated facing may not meet this — verify the listing before specifying. ASJ (all-service jacket) facings are typically 25/50; FSK (foil-scrim-kraft) may or may not be, depending on the product.

**Exam Trap:** Pipe insulation thickness requirements are **per IECC, not IMC**. The IMC defers to the IECC for energy-related insulation. On the exam, a question about hydronic insulation R-value or thickness is testing IECC knowledge applied through the IMC reference.`,

    "Pressure Relief & Pipe Materials": `Hydronic systems require **pressure relief valves (PRVs)** per IMC 1006 — same general rules as boilers. A PRV must be installed on every closed system, sized to discharge the full input capacity at the relief setpoint, and **no shutoff valve between the system and the PRV**. The PRV discharge must terminate at a **safe location** (floor drain or outdoors) where hot water discharge won't injure occupants or damage property. The PRV setpoint must be **at or below the MAWP** of the lowest-rated component in the system — typically 30 psi for residential hot water boilers, higher for commercial.

**Approved hydronic pipe materials** per IMC 1202 include **steel** (black or galvanized), **copper** (Type K, L, or M), **brass**, **CPVC** (for hot water up to 200°F), **PEX** (for both heating and cooling, with appropriate temperature/pressure ratings), and **PE-RT** (polyethylene of raised temperature). **PVC is NOT permitted** for heated hydronic systems — PVC's temperature rating is too low. **Joining methods** include welded/brazed/soldered/threaded for metal, solvent-cement for CPVC, crimp/expansion/press fittings for PEX. Pipe in concrete slabs (radiant heat) must be **continuous from manifold to return manifold** with no fittings buried in the slab. **Manifolds** must be accessible for service.

**Exam Trap:** **PVC is not permitted for hot water hydronic systems** — only CPVC is rated for the temperatures. PVC's maximum service temperature is around 140°F (and even that derates pressure substantially); CPVC handles up to 200°F. Using PVC on a hot water heating line will fail catastrophically when the boiler comes up to temperature.

**Exam Trap:** **No fittings in the slab** for radiant heating. The PEX or other tubing must be continuous from the manifold, looped through the slab pattern, and back to the manifold with all fittings above the slab where they can be accessed. A buried fitting that leaks requires breaking the slab to repair — a disaster.`
  },

  14: {
    "One- & Two-Family Dwelling Scope": `Class B (F32) Residential supervisor scope is **strictly limited to one- and two-family dwellings, IRC-compliant townhomes, and Group U (utility/miscellaneous) occupancies**. The IRC (International Residential Code) covers these buildings; the IBC covers everything else. A Class B holder cannot pull permits for: **apartment buildings of 3 units or more**, **hotels**, **motels**, **bed-and-breakfast establishments with more than 5 guest rooms**, **dormitories**, **assisted living**, **commercial**, **industrial**, or **assembly** occupancies. The limit is by **occupancy classification**, not by physical size — a 6,000 sq ft single-family home is still Class B scope; a 1,500 sq ft duplex with three units (illegal conversion or not) is Class A scope.

The IRC mechanical chapter (M1300 series) is largely a simplified version of the IMC adapted for residential use. Many IRC residential rules **defer to the IMC** for technical details (e.g., combustion air for high-input appliances refers back to IFGC). Where the IRC has its own residential-specific rule (such as residential ventilation in M1505 — total whole-house ventilation per ASHRAE 62.2), that rule controls for residential. **Detached accessory structures** (sheds, garages) are typically **Group U** and within Class B scope unless they exceed certain size or contain commercial activity.

**Exam Trap:** **A duplex (two-family) is within Class B scope; a triplex is NOT.** The line is drawn at two dwelling units. Once you hit three units, you're under the IBC and need Class A. Many candidates assume "small multifamily" is residential — only **one and two-unit** structures qualify.

**Exam Trap:** **An IRC townhome must be a single dwelling unit from foundation to roof** with no dwelling units above or below — separated from adjacent townhomes by an IRC-rated party wall. A "townhome" with a basement apartment is no longer an IRC townhome and falls under the IBC. Verify the building classification before assuming Class B scope.`,

    "5-Ton A/C & 100 MBtu Limits": `Two hard equipment limits define Class B scope: **air conditioning ≤ 5 tons (60,000 Btu/hr) cooling capacity** and **water heaters ≤ 100,000 Btu/hr (100 MBtu) input**. These are absolute caps — there is no provision for "slightly over" or "rounded down." A **6-ton split system** in a 4,000 sq ft single-family home requires a **Class A supervisor** to pull the permit even though the building is residential. A **120 MBtu tankless water heater** likewise pushes a project out of Class B scope. The limits are based on **manufacturer's nameplate rating**, not on calculated load — even if the system rarely runs at full capacity, the nameplate controls.

The 5-ton limit translates roughly to **homes under 3,000–3,500 sq ft** in most climates, depending on insulation and orientation. Higher-end custom homes and large additions frequently exceed 5 tons. **Two smaller systems totaling more than 5 tons** are still within Class B scope as long as each individual unit is ≤5 tons — the limit is per **single unit**, not aggregate. This is a common workaround for upper-end residential projects. The 100 MBtu water heater limit applies per single appliance — two **80 MBtu** units serving the same home are within scope, while one **120 MBtu** unit is not.

**Exam Trap:** The **5-ton and 100 MBtu limits are per-unit, not aggregate**. A 4,000 sq ft home with **two 4-ton split systems (8 tons total)** is within Class B scope; the same home with **one 6-ton unit** is not. Students assume "total tonnage" controls — it does not.

**Exam Trap:** **Nameplate rating, not calculated load**, controls. A heat pump rated **5.5 tons** that the Manual J load shows only needs 3 tons of capacity is still **over** the Class B 5-ton limit. The cap is on equipment size, not on system performance.`,

    "Residential Heat Pumps & Furnaces": `Residential heat pumps (air-source, ground-source, water-source) follow IMC and IRC requirements plus manufacturer's listing. **Air-source heat pumps** in Denver's climate zone 5B must be sized for **heating load**, not just cooling, because heat pump capacity drops as outdoor temperature falls — at design heating temperature (Denver: roughly **1°F**), an air-source heat pump may deliver only **40–60%** of its 47°F rated capacity. **Supplemental electric resistance heat** (heat strips, typically 5 kW or 10 kW per stage) or a **dual-fuel gas furnace backup** is required to meet the design heating load. The **balance point** is the outdoor temperature at which heat pump capacity equals heating load — below that, supplemental heat fills the gap.

Residential gas furnaces are typically **Category I (atmospheric)** or **Category IV (condensing 90%+ AFUE)**. Category IV furnaces require **PVC or polypropylene venting** through a sidewall or roof, **condensate drainage** (Category IV produces acidic condensate that should drain through a neutralizer for some installations), and **outdoor combustion air intake**. Furnace **clearance to combustibles** is per the manufacturer's listing — typically **0 inches on the sides and back** for cabinet-style units and **6 inches at the top** for Type B vent. The **filter must be accessible for service** and properly sized to the manufacturer's pressure drop tolerance.

**Exam Trap:** Air-source heat pumps in cold climates **require supplemental heat**. A heat pump sized to meet design heating load at 47°F will be 50% short at 1°F outdoor temperature. The exam will sometimes describe a heat pump installation with no supplemental heat in a cold climate — that's a design failure even if all the components are individually code-compliant.

**Exam Trap:** **Category IV furnace condensate is mildly acidic** (pH 3–5 from dissolved CO2 forming carbonic acid). Discharging condensate into copper drain piping over time causes corrosion. PVC or ABS condensate piping is preferred; some jurisdictions require a **condensate neutralizer** (limestone media) before discharge to drain.`
  },

  15: {
    "Tankless vs Tank": `Water heaters come in two main configurations: **tank-type** (storage water heaters with a 30–80 gallon insulated tank, heated by gas burner or electric elements) and **tankless** (on-demand heaters that heat water only when flow is detected, no storage). **Tank-type gas water heaters** are typically **40 MBH for a 40-gallon unit**, **65 MBH for a 75-gallon unit**, and use either **atmospheric (Category I) venting through Type B vent or a chimney**, **power vent (small fan, sidewall PVC)**, or **direct vent (sealed combustion, sidewall coaxial)**. **Tankless gas water heaters** are typically **180,000 to 199,000 Btu/hr input** (just under 200 MBH for residential code compliance), Category IV condensing, with PVC sidewall venting. Tankless units provide unlimited hot water at a given flow rate but cannot handle simultaneous high-flow demands like tank units.

For Class B scope, the **100 MBtu input limit** rules out almost all tankless gas water heaters — most tankless units exceed 100 MBH and require a Class A supervisor for permit. **Electric tank water heaters** (240V, typically 4500W elements, 18.75 amps at 240V) have no input limit issue since the limit is on Btu/hr input — not electric kW (although the electric circuit must be sized per NEC 422). **Heat pump water heaters** (hybrid) draw electricity to run a small heat pump that extracts heat from surrounding air; they are extremely efficient but require **at least 1,000 cubic feet of surrounding air** for the heat pump to operate effectively.

**Exam Trap:** **Most tankless gas water heaters exceed Class B scope.** A 199 MBH tankless unit requires a Class A (F29) supervisor for permit even in a single-family home. Class B is capped at **100 MBtu input** per appliance. Recommend tank-type or two smaller tankless units (each ≤100 MBH) for Class B scope projects.

**Exam Trap:** **Heat pump water heaters need surrounding air volume**. Installing a heat pump water heater in a small closet (less than 1,000 cubic feet) will cause the unit to ice up, run inefficiently, or shift to resistance-element mode (defeating the efficiency purpose). The installation must provide adequate air or be ducted to a larger space.`,

    "T&P Valve & Drain Requirements": `Every water heater must have a **temperature and pressure (T&P) relief valve** per IMC 504 and IPC 504. The T&P valve has two functions in one: relieves pressure if it exceeds the valve setpoint (typically **150 psi** for residential) and relieves temperature if water exceeds **210°F**. The valve protects against **runaway heating** (a stuck thermostat or gas valve that could superheat the water until it flashes to steam and ruptures the tank). T&P valve **discharge piping** must be:

- **Same diameter as the valve outlet** (typically 3/4 inch — never reduced)
- **Sloped downward continuously to drain**
- **Terminate within 6 inches of the floor** or to an approved indirect waste receptor
- **Of approved material** rated for the discharge temperature (typically copper, CPVC, or galvanized — not PEX in most jurisdictions, due to temperature ratings of PEX fittings)
- **NOT trapped, threaded at the discharge end, or fitted with a shutoff valve**

The discharge must terminate where it is **visible** so an occupant notices when the valve discharges (signaling a malfunction). Drain pans under tank water heaters are required per IPC when leakage would cause damage to building components — typical pan is 1-1/2 inches deep with a **3/4 inch drain piped to floor drain or to an indirect waste**. Some jurisdictions require an **automatic shutoff valve** on the cold water supply to a water heater in a finished space, triggered by a leak detector in the drain pan.

**Exam Trap:** The **T&P discharge cannot be reduced in size, trapped, threaded, or shut off**. Inspectors specifically look for these violations — a discharge that ends in male NPT threads (so a homeowner could cap it) is a fail. The discharge must be a smooth pipe ending within 6 inches of the floor, visible and open.

**Exam Trap:** **PEX is generally not rated for T&P discharge piping** because the brass fittings can fail at high temperatures (PEX itself is rated for 200°F at 80 psi but fittings derate at temperature). Use copper, CPVC, or galvanized for T&P discharge unless the local AHJ specifically approves PEX with appropriate fittings.`,

    "Heat Pump Water Heaters": `**Heat pump water heaters (HPWHs)** use a vapor-compression cycle to extract heat from surrounding air and concentrate it into the water tank — typically delivering **2.5 to 3.5 units of heat per unit of electric input** (COP of 2.5–3.5), versus 1.0 for a standard electric resistance water heater. HPWHs are the most efficient electric water heater available and qualify for many utility rebates. The unit looks like a standard 50- or 80-gallon electric water heater with a **fan, evaporator, and compressor on top of the tank**. Most HPWHs also have **backup electric resistance elements** that engage when hot water demand exceeds heat pump capacity (e.g., a large guest visit) or when ambient temperature falls below the heat pump's operating range.

Installation requirements per manufacturer and IMC: at least **1,000 cubic feet of unobstructed air around the unit** (a typical mechanical closet is way too small without ducted air), **operating ambient temperature 40°F to 120°F** (heat pump won't run in cold garage), **condensate drain** (the evaporator pulls humidity from the air, producing condensate that must be drained), and **electrical 240V/30A circuit** typical. HPWHs cool and dehumidify the surrounding space — beneficial in a hot, humid basement (where the cooling is useful); problematic in a cold, dry basement (where extracting heat reduces the basement's heating system capacity). Best location: a large basement or unconditioned garage where ambient is consistently above 40°F.

**Exam Trap:** **HPWHs cool the space they're in.** A 50-gallon HPWH extracts about **5,000 Btu/hr** from the surrounding air when operating. In a small closet, this drops the air temperature, reducing heat pump efficiency, and eventually the unit shifts to inefficient resistance-element mode. Always provide adequate air volume (1,000 cu ft min) or duct intake/exhaust to a larger space.

**Exam Trap:** **HPWH condensate drain is mandatory** because the evaporator generates condensate just like an A/C coil. Installations that omit the condensate drain will eventually flood the surrounding floor when the float switch trips (or worse, if there's no float switch).`
  },

  16: {
    "Pipe Sizing Method (Table 402.4)": `Gas pipe sizing per IFGC Chapter 4 uses **Table 402.4** (or equivalent series tables) to size each section of gas piping based on **total cubic feet per hour (cfh) of flow**, **developed length to the most remote appliance**, and **inlet pressure and allowable pressure drop**. The basic procedure for a low-pressure system (0.5 psig, 0.5" w.c. pressure drop, natural gas with **1,025 Btu/cf** heating value):

1. **Calculate cfh per appliance**: Appliance input (Btu/hr) ÷ heating value (Btu/cf) = cfh. A 100,000 Btu/hr furnace = **100,000 ÷ 1,025 ≈ 98 cfh**.
2. **Sum cfh for all appliances** served by each pipe section. The main line carries the total of all downstream demand.
3. **Measure developed length** from the meter (or regulator outlet) to the **most remote appliance**. Use this length for ALL pipe sections (the "longest-length method") — this is conservative but always safe.
4. **Look up minimum pipe size** in the appropriate table for the section's cfh and the longest-length value.

For a hotel kitchen with a **400 MBH cooking range, 250 MBH dishwasher booster, and a 199 MBH water heater** (total ~849 MBH ≈ 829 cfh) at 80 feet from the meter, the main line consults Table 402.4 at **800 cfh row, 80 ft column** and reads a pipe diameter — typically **1-1/2 to 2 inch** depending on the specific table edition. Each branch is then sized for its own cfh at the longest-length value (80 ft).

**Key Formula:** cfh = **(Appliance input Btu/hr) ÷ (Heating value Btu/cf)**. Natural gas ≈ **1,025 Btu/cf**; propane ≈ **2,520 Btu/cf**.

**Exam Trap:** Students use the **actual length of each branch** instead of the **longest developed length to the most remote appliance**. The IFGC "longest-length method" uses the longest distance for ALL sections — it's intentionally conservative. The alternative "branch-length method" allows section-by-section sizing but is more complex and rarely tested.

**Exam Trap:** Different tables apply for **propane vs natural gas**. Propane has roughly **2.5x the heating value per cubic foot** of natural gas, so a propane line can be smaller for the same Btu/hr demand. Using a natural gas table for propane oversizes the pipe (wasteful but safe); using a propane table for natural gas undersizes (dangerous — appliances starve). Verify the gas type before sizing.`,

    "Materials & Joining": `IFGC 403 lists approved gas piping materials. **Black steel pipe** (Schedule 40 carbon steel, threaded or welded) is the workhorse for indoor gas piping. **Copper tubing** is permitted for natural gas in some jurisdictions where the gas is not corrosive (most utility gas is conditioned to remove sulfur); copper for propane is more restricted because some propane contains compounds that attack copper. **CSST (Corrugated Stainless Steel Tubing)** is permitted with proper bonding (see next article). **Polyethylene (PE)** is permitted **outdoors and underground only** — never inside a building. **PVC and CPVC are NOT permitted** for gas piping. **Galvanized steel** is permitted but the zinc coating can flake off into the gas stream — black steel is preferred.

Joining methods: **threaded** (steel pipe) with appropriate **pipe sealant** rated for gas service (Teflon tape rated for gas, or pipe dope listed for gas), **welded** (steel, requires qualified welder), **brazed or soldered** (copper, with **filler metal having a melting point above 1,000°F** per IFGC 403.10), **flared** (copper to flare fittings, no solder), **press fittings** (listed for gas service per CSA standards), and **mechanical CSST fittings**. **PE pipe** is joined by **heat fusion** (butt fusion or socket fusion) or **electrofusion** — these methods require qualified operators.

**Exam Trap:** **Solder for gas piping copper must have a melting point above 1,000°F.** Standard 50/50 tin-lead solder melts around 460°F and is not approved for gas. Silver brazing (BAg alloys, melting around 1,200°F) is required. Using common plumbing solder on a gas joint is a serious violation.

**Exam Trap:** **PE pipe is for outdoor/underground only**. A PE-to-steel transition fitting must be made **above grade outside the building** before the pipe enters. Running PE underground all the way into the building basement is a violation because PE is not rated for indoor use (UV resistance, fire performance, mechanical protection).`,

    "Sediment Traps, Valves, CSST Bonding": `Three key gas piping accessories are heavily tested. **Sediment traps (drip legs)** per IFGC 408.4 must be installed **upstream of the appliance gas connection** at each appliance EXCEPT: **ranges**, **clothes dryers**, **illuminating appliances (gas lights)**, **outdoor grills**, and **decorative gas log appliances**. The trap is typically a **3-inch nipple capped at the bottom**, installed as a tee where the gas flow enters through the side and the trap extends downward — debris and condensate fall into the trap rather than carrying through to the appliance valve.

**Shutoff valves** per IFGC 409.5 must be installed **within 6 feet of each appliance, in the same room, accessible without removing permanent construction**. A valve in the basement that serves a second-floor furnace is a violation even if it's the only valve in the system — there must be a local valve within 6 feet of the furnace. The valve must be a **listed gas valve** (typically a quarter-turn ball valve with a yellow handle for visibility).

**CSST bonding** per IFGC 310.1 (and NEC 250.104(B)) requires the gas piping system to be bonded with a **minimum 6 AWG copper bonding conductor** attached to **a brass fitting on the CSST manifold** (NOT to the CSST tubing itself). The bonding jumper connects to the building's grounding electrode system. The purpose is to dissipate **lightning-induced surge currents** that have been documented to puncture CSST and cause gas leaks/fires. Bonding is required even if the CSST is a small portion of the gas system — any CSST anywhere in the building triggers the requirement.

**Exam Trap:** **Drip leg NOT required for ranges, dryers, illuminating, outdoor grills, decorative log appliances.** Furnaces, boilers, water heaters, unit heaters, and most other appliances DO require drip legs. Memorize the exception list — it's narrow and consistently tested.

**Exam Trap:** **CSST bonding is 6 AWG copper to the brass fitting**, not 8 AWG, not to the CSST tubing itself, and not the same as the equipment grounding conductor. This is the #6 item on the top 12 HVAC exam traps list.`
  },

  17: {
    "Hood Sizing & Overhang": `Commercial kitchen hood sizing is governed by IMC Chapter 5 and NFPA 96. A **Type I hood** (grease-laden vapors) must overhang the cooking surface by **at least 6 inches on all open sides** to capture the rising thermal plume of grease vapors before it escapes. **Type II hoods** (heat/steam) have less strict overhang requirements but follow similar capture principles. Hood length is typically the **cooking appliance length plus the overhang on each open side**. For a **48-inch wide fryer battery against a wall** (one open side at each end, none at the back wall), the hood is **48 + 6 + 6 = 60 inches long** minimum.

**Hood exhaust airflow** depends on **hood style and cooking appliance type**. **Wall-mounted canopy hoods** over heavy-duty appliances (charbroilers, woks) require **300–400 cfm per linear foot of hood length**. **Light-duty appliances** (ovens, steam tables) require **200–250 cfm/ft**. **Island canopy hoods** require higher rates (typically 400 cfm/ft or more) because they have four open sides. **Make-up air** per IMC 508 must be supplied at a rate that keeps the building at no greater than **0.02 inches w.c. negative pressure** relative to outdoors — typically **80–90% of exhaust airflow** is delivered as make-up air. The remaining **10–20%** is "borrowed" from the building's general ventilation.

**Exam Trap:** **6-inch overhang on open sides only.** A hood mounted against a wall (one closed side) needs 6 inches of overhang on the two ends and the front — not on the wall side. Don't oversize by extending overhang to closed sides.

**Exam Trap:** **Type I hood exhaust airflow** varies by cooking appliance duty rating. A wall-mount over a charbroiler needs significantly more cfm/ft than the same hood over a steam table. Using a generic 200 cfm/ft for all hoods over heavy-duty equipment results in inadequate capture and grease accumulation in the duct.`,

    "Make-up Air Requirements": `Per IMC 508, commercial kitchen hood exhaust must be balanced by **make-up air (MUA)** to prevent excessive negative building pressure. The code requirement is **performance-based**: the **building cannot exceed 0.02 in. w.c. negative pressure** relative to outdoors (IMC 508.1.1), and MUA must be sized to **approximately equal the exhaust**. In practice, designers deliver **80–90% of exhaust airflow as dedicated tempered MUA** and let the remaining **10–20%** come from transfer air (dining-room overflow, building general ventilation). **There is no fixed percentage in the IMC** — the 80/90% range is design convention, not a code threshold. What the code DOES fix is the 0.02-in. w.c. negative-pressure ceiling. MUA is typically **tempered**: heated to at least 60°F in winter and cooled/dehumidified in summer in Denver climate, so the kitchen remains workable. Untempered MUA dumped directly into the kitchen creates cold drafts in winter and humidity in summer — and workers will close it off, defeating the system.

**MUA distribution strategies**: **transfer air from adjacent dining rooms** (allowed up to a portion of total MUA, but cannot exceed the dining room's design ventilation), **direct supply at the hood face** (ceiling-mounted diffusers near the hood), or **perforated perimeter supply** along the kitchen walls. The MUA fan must be **electrically interlocked** with the hood exhaust fan — when the hood comes on, MUA comes on automatically. **Variable-air-volume hood controls** modulate exhaust and MUA together based on cooking activity (Demand Control Kitchen Ventilation, DCKV), reducing energy waste during slow periods.

**Exam Trap:** **MUA fan must be interlocked with the hood exhaust fan.** Operating the exhaust without MUA creates extreme negative pressure that can backdraft water heaters, boilers, and any other atmospheric-vented gas appliances in the building — pulling combustion products into occupied spaces. The interlock prevents this and is a hard code requirement.

**Exam Trap:** **MUA cannot be 100% from transfer air** in most installations. The kitchen's exhaust often exceeds the dining room's design ventilation rate, so dedicated MUA from outdoors is required for the balance. Borrowing all the MUA from dining transfer creates a chain of negative pressure that destabilizes the building.

**Exam Trap:** The "90% MUA" figure is a **design rule of thumb, not a code number**. IMC 508 sets a **0.02-in. w.c. negative-pressure limit** and requires MUA "approximately equal" to exhaust — the percentage split between dedicated MUA and transfer air is design judgment. Don't memorize "90%" as a code requirement; memorize the 0.02-in. w.c. ceiling.`,

    "Gas Connections to Cooking Equipment": `Per IFGC 411 and NFPA 96, **commercial cooking appliances** that must be moved for cleaning behind/under (most ranges, fryers, griddles in commercial kitchens) require a **listed flexible gas appliance connector**. The connector must be **listed for commercial cooking equipment** (typically a stainless-steel hose with a quick-disconnect fitting) and must be installed with a **restraining cable or strap** that prevents the appliance from rolling far enough to stress the gas connection. The connector length is limited to **6 feet** maximum for most listings. **Permanent (rigid) gas piping** is permitted but is uncommon for movable cooking equipment because it prevents cleaning underneath.

**Quick-disconnect fittings** allow the appliance to be unplugged for service without tools. The disconnect must be **listed for the gas type and pressure** and must **automatically shut off the gas** when disconnected (to prevent leaks if the appliance is removed for repair). A **manual shutoff valve** must be installed **within 6 feet of the appliance** and **upstream** of the connector — typically mounted on the wall or a service column, accessible without moving the appliance. **Sediment trap is NOT required** for ranges (they're in the IFGC 408.4 exception list), but **IS required** for water heaters and gas-fired booster heaters in the kitchen.

**Exam Trap:** **Restraining cable required on movable commercial cooking equipment.** A range on casters with a flexible gas connector but no restraining cable is a violation — the cable prevents the range from being pulled too far and stressing/breaking the gas connector. Inspectors specifically check for the cable.

**Exam Trap:** **Rigid gas piping is permitted but impractical for movable equipment.** If a question asks what's required, the answer is **listed flexible connector with restraining cable AND manual shutoff valve within 6 feet upstream**. Rigid piping satisfies the code but defeats the cleaning purpose.`
  },

  18: {
    "2024 DBC Adoption & Permit Process": `Denver adopted the **2025 Denver Building Code (DBC)** effective **2025-06-13**, which is based on the **2024 I-Codes** (including 2024 IMC, 2024 IFGC, 2024 IECC, 2024 IBC, 2024 IRC) with Denver-specific amendments. The 2025 DBC applies to **all new building permits filed on or after the adoption date**. Permits filed before are processed under the **2018 DBC** (based on 2018 I-Codes). Mechanical/HVAC permits in Denver are pulled through **Denver Community Planning & Development (CPD)** via the e-permits system at denvergov.org. A licensed Denver mechanical supervisor (Class A, B, or Gas) must be on the permit as the responsible party.

The **permit process for a commercial mechanical installation** typically involves: (1) submitting plans stamped by a Colorado-licensed mechanical engineer for projects above a size threshold (or sealed by the supervisor for smaller jobs), (2) plan review by CPD with comments and revisions, (3) permit issuance with fee payment, (4) installation with required inspections (typically rough-in, pre-cover, and final), (5) pressure testing for gas systems before backfilling/cover, and (6) commissioning and final inspection approval. **Hotel mechanical systems** are typically reviewed under **commercial building** classification with all the associated stamped drawings, energy compliance documentation (COMcheck), and ventilation per ASHRAE 62.1.

**Denver Mechanical Permit — process gates that ARE testable** (fees change; process does not):

1. **License & qualifier on file.** The Class A/B/Gas supervisor must have a current Denver CPD certificate before the permit can issue. New companies must register at denvergov.org and have the qualifier on file 5–10 business days before applying.
2. **Plan review threshold.** Commercial mechanical projects above the Denver-set threshold (currently any project requiring a building permit + new equipment exceeding ~50,000 Btu/hr or any new gas piping) require **stamped engineering drawings** from a Colorado-licensed PE. Smaller jobs may be permitted on a supervisor-sealed drawing.
3. **Inspection sequence.** Typical Denver mechanical inspection card: **(a) gas rough-in / pressure test**, **(b) duct rough / pre-cover**, **(c) appliance final / vent termination / combustion air verification**. Gas pressure test is **10 psi for 15 minutes minimum** (Denver amendment; IFGC base is 3 psi minimum), with **no observable pressure loss**.
4. **State boiler registration.** Pressure vessels and steam/HW boilers above the state threshold register separately with **Colorado Division of Oil and Public Safety, Boiler Section** — a state filing distinct from the Denver building permit.
5. **Fees.** Denver mechanical permit fees are set by ordinance and updated annually; consult **denvergov.org/permits** at time of application. As a planning rule of thumb, mechanical fees on a typical commercial fit-out run **1–2% of mechanical project cost** with a minimum fee, plus separate plan-review and inspection fees.

**Exam Trap:** The **exam edition often lags the field adoption**. ICC G29 is based on **2018 IMC/IFGC**, while Denver field permits are based on **2024 IMC/IFGC** as of 2025-06-13. Section numbers and some specific requirements changed between editions. Pass the exam on **2018 code**, then update to **2024 code** for actual field practice.

**Exam Trap:** **A licensed supervisor must be named on the permit.** A general contractor without a mechanical supervisor cannot pull a mechanical permit, even if the GC is licensed for the overall project. Denver CPD checks the mechanical supervisor's certificate on file before issuing.`,

    "Climate Adaptations (Snow, Freeze, High-Altitude)": `Denver's climate (zone 5B, semi-arid, elevation ~5,280 ft) drives several mechanical code amendments not found in the base IMC. **Snow load** affects equipment platforms, outdoor unit mounting, and intake/exhaust terminations. Rooftop equipment must be elevated to keep **the equipment base above maximum design snow depth** (typically **18 inches minimum** for Denver, more in higher elevations). Outdoor intake louvers must be located **above expected snow accumulation** to prevent blockage — typical bottom-of-intake elevation is **18 inches above grade or roof surface**, more in heavy snow areas.

**Freeze protection** is critical for hydronic systems with any pipe exposed to unconditioned space — outdoor portions of condenser water loops, makeup water lines through exterior walls, condensate drains in cold attics. Solutions include **heat trace cable with thermostatic control**, **glycol antifreeze** (typically 30–40% propylene glycol for HVAC, lower for potable systems), and **drain-down provisions** for shutdown periods. **High altitude** (Denver at 5,280 ft) reduces atmospheric pressure to about **12.2 psi vs 14.7 psi at sea level** — combustion equipment must be **derated for altitude per manufacturer's instructions**, typically **4% per 1,000 ft above sea level**, so Denver-installed equipment runs at roughly **80%** of its sea-level Btu rating without adjustment. Gas appliance orifices and burner adjustments must be sized for altitude.

**Exam Trap:** **Altitude derating** is a real, testable requirement. A **100,000 Btu/hr** natural gas furnace at Denver elevation (5,280 ft) without altitude adjustment produces only about **80,000 Btu/hr** of input — 20% less than the nameplate. The manufacturer's installation instructions include high-altitude conversion kits (smaller orifices) that must be installed for proper operation.

**Exam Trap:** **Outdoor combustion air intakes can be blocked by snow** in Denver. The IFGC 304.6.4 doesn't specify a snow-clearance dimension, but Denver amendments and good practice require the **bottom of the intake to be at least 18 inches above maximum snow depth**. A 1-foot snow event that buries a low intake will shut down (or worse, cause CO production from) gas appliances.`,

    "Denver vs IMC Base Code Differences": `Several Denver amendments diverge meaningfully from the base IMC/IFGC. **Tight residential envelope** amendments require **outdoor combustion air for almost all new-construction fuel-burning appliances** — Denver does not allow the indoor air method for new residential mechanical installations because envelope tightness from modern construction (post-2009 IECC) does not provide adequate infiltration for the 50 cu ft / 1,000 Btu/hr rule. Direct-vent and sealed-combustion appliances are strongly preferred. **Boiler permits and inspections** are coordinated between Denver CPD (building permit) and the **Colorado Division of Oil and Public Safety boiler section** (state boiler inspection registration). Most commercial boilers and pressure vessels above certain thresholds must be registered with the state.

**Refrigerant machinery room** rules in the DBC cross-reference the IBC for **occupancy classification** of the machinery room — high-hazard occupancies (B occupancy with toxic refrigerants) trigger additional fire-rated separation and emergency systems beyond the base IMC. **Wildfire protection** zones in Denver foothill areas have additional exterior vent termination and roof-mounted equipment requirements not found in the base code. **Energy code (IECC) amendments** in Denver are typically more stringent than the base IECC — verify ASHRAE 90.1 compliance paths and COMcheck submittals for commercial work. **Plan review fees** and **permit fees** are set by Denver ordinance and updated periodically — verify current fees at the time of permit application.

**Exam Trap:** **Denver effectively prohibits indoor air method combustion air** for new residential construction due to envelope tightness amendments. A new single-family home with a closet-installed Category I gas water heater requires **two openings to outdoors** (or a direct-vent appliance) — the 50 cu ft / 1,000 Btu/hr indoor method is not accepted by Denver inspectors even if the math works on paper.

**Exam Trap:** **State boiler registration** is separate from the Denver building permit. A commercial boiler that received a Denver mechanical permit still needs to be registered with the Colorado Division of Oil and Public Safety and inspected by a state-certified boiler inspector on the required schedule. Skipping the state registration is a separate violation even with the building permit complete.`
  },

  19: {
    "Clean Air Act §608 & Ozone Science": `**Section 608 of the Clean Air Act** (1990 Amendments) prohibits **knowingly venting** any ozone-depleting refrigerant or substitute during the service, maintenance, repair, or disposal of refrigeration and air conditioning equipment. The original scope covered **CFCs (chlorofluorocarbons)** and **HCFCs (hydrochlorofluorocarbons)**. Effective **November 15, 2019**, EPA extended the §608 venting prohibition to **HFCs (hydrofluorocarbons)** — meaning today's R-410A, R-134a, R-404A, R-32, and similar refrigerants are subject to the same venting prohibition that originally targeted R-12 and R-22. The prohibition applies to **any technician handling refrigerant**, and violations carry **civil penalties up to $44,539 per day per violation** (2023 adjusted figure).

**Ozone depletion science**: Stratospheric ozone (O3) absorbs UV-B radiation that would otherwise reach Earth's surface and cause skin cancer, cataracts, and ecosystem damage. CFCs released at the surface are stable enough to drift into the stratosphere where UV radiation breaks them apart, releasing **chlorine atoms**. Each chlorine atom catalytically destroys **approximately 100,000 ozone molecules** before being deactivated. **Ozone Depletion Potential (ODP)** is a relative scale with **CFC-12 = 1.0** as the reference. HCFCs have **lower ODP** (R-22 ODP = 0.05) but are still phased out under Montreal Protocol. **HFCs have ODP = 0** (no chlorine) — they don't deplete ozone, but they ARE potent **greenhouse gases** with high Global Warming Potential (GWP). HFC phasedown is the focus of the **AIM Act (2020)** rather than §608 directly.

**Exam Trap:** **HFCs ARE covered by §608 venting prohibition** as of November 2019 — many older training materials say "HFCs are not regulated" which was true before 2019. The current rule prohibits venting **all refrigerants and substitutes**, including HFCs, A2L blends, and HFOs.

**Exam Trap:** **ODP and GWP are different metrics.** ODP measures stratospheric ozone destruction. GWP measures global warming contribution per pound of release. HFCs have **ODP = 0** but **GWP that can exceed 4,000**. Both metrics matter, but §608 venting prohibition was originally written for ODP and now extended to all refrigerants regardless of metric.`,

    "Refrigerant Classifications (CFC, HCFC, HFC, A2L)": `Refrigerants are classified by **chemical family** (which determines environmental impact) and by **ASHRAE 34 safety classification** (which determines handling requirements). **Chemical families**: **CFC** (chlorofluorocarbon — chlorine, fluorine, carbon; high ODP; production phased out under Montreal Protocol effective 1996 for new equipment) — examples: **R-12 (auto AC), R-11 (chillers)**. **HCFC** (hydrochlorofluorocarbon — adds hydrogen, lower ODP; phasedown under Montreal Protocol; new equipment production ban 2010; refrigerant production ban 2020) — example: **R-22**. **HFC** (hydrofluorocarbon — no chlorine, ODP = 0; high GWP; phasedown under AIM Act) — examples: **R-410A, R-134a, R-404A, R-32**. **HFO** (hydrofluoroolefin — low GWP) — examples: **R-1234yf, R-1234ze**. **Natural refrigerants** (CO2 R-744, ammonia R-717, propane R-290) — zero or near-zero GWP and ODP.

**ASHRAE 34 safety classification** has two letters and a number. **Letter (toxicity)**: **A** = lower toxicity; **B** = higher toxicity. **Number (flammability)**: **1** = no flame propagation; **2L** = lower flammability (burning velocity ≤ 10 cm/s); **2** = lower flammability; **3** = higher flammability. Common classifications: **A1** (R-22, R-410A, R-134a, R-404A — lowest hazard); **A2L** (R-32, R-454B, R-1234yf — mildly flammable, restricted but increasingly common); **A3** (R-290 propane — highly flammable, small charge limits only); **B1** (R-123 — toxic but non-flammable); **B2L** (R-717 ammonia — moderately toxic, mildly flammable).

**Exam Trap:** **R-22 is HCFC, R-410A is HFC.** They are NOT both HCFCs. R-22 production was banned in 2020 (the chlorine is the ozone-depleting concern). R-410A has no chlorine but very high GWP and is being phased down under AIM Act. Don't lump them together.

**Exam Trap:** **A2L is not "non-flammable."** A2L refrigerants WILL burn under sufficient ignition energy and concentration. They are "mildly flammable" with low burning velocity, but installation rules (charge limits, ventilation, ignition source restrictions) apply. Treating A2L as if it were A1 will fail safety questions on the exam.`,

    "Recovery, Recycling, Reclamation": `Three different processes are tested as definitions and as procedural requirements. **Recovery**: removing refrigerant from a system in any condition and storing it in an external container. Recovery does NOT clean the refrigerant — the recovered material may contain moisture, air, oil, and other contaminants. Recovery is required whenever refrigerant is removed from a system for service, replacement, or disposal. **Recycling**: cleaning recovered refrigerant **on-site** for reuse in the same owner's equipment, using oil separation and a single-pass filter-drier. Recycled refrigerant is acceptable for reuse but does not meet AHRI 700 purity specifications. **Reclamation**: cleaning refrigerant to meet **AHRI Standard 700 new-product purity** using distillation and chemical analysis. Reclamation is performed at certified **reclamation facilities**, not in the field. Reclaimed refrigerant can be sold as new product.

**Recovery equipment** used since **November 15, 1993** must be **EPA-certified** to meet specific recovery efficiency standards. The certification level depends on the refrigerant type — Type I (small appliance), Type II (high-pressure), Type III (low-pressure). The recovery efficiency required is specified per equipment type and refrigerant. Recovery must continue until the system pressure reaches the **required vacuum level** (see the Type II/III modules for specifics).

**Recordkeeping**: Technicians must maintain **certification records** for each technician handling refrigerant. **Sales records** of refrigerant in containers larger than **2 pounds** must show the buyer's §608 certification (the **sales restriction** ensures only certified technicians purchase refrigerant). **Disposal records** for appliances must show that refrigerant was recovered before the appliance was sent to scrap — the **final person in the disposal chain** is responsible for ensuring recovery has occurred or for performing it themselves.

**Exam Trap:** **Recycling and reclamation are different.** Recycling cleans on-site for reuse in the same owner's system. Reclamation cleans to new-product purity at a certified facility. A technician can recycle in the field but cannot reclaim — reclamation is a regulated facility process.

**Exam Trap:** **Sales restriction is for containers larger than 2 pounds** of refrigerant. Single-use small cans (typically 1 lb of auto AC refrigerant) are exempt from the sales restriction (though that loophole has been narrowed for some refrigerants by state-level rules, especially in California). The federal exemption is at the **2-pound threshold**.`
  },

  20: {
    "5-Pound Factory-Sealed Rule": `**Type I certification** under EPA §608 covers **small appliances**, defined as products **manufactured, charged, and hermetically sealed in a factory** with **5 pounds or less** of refrigerant. The classic Type I appliances are **household refrigerators, freezers, window air conditioners, packaged terminal air conditioners (PTACs), dehumidifiers, water coolers, and vending machines**. The defining characteristics are: factory-sealed (not field-charged), small charge (≤5 lb), and hermetic (no service valves accessible without breaking a brazed joint).

A unit that was originally factory-sealed but later **field-modified with service valves** for retrofit refrigerant change is **no longer a Type I appliance** — it has become a Type II appliance and requires Type II certification to service. A **packaged rooftop air conditioner** charged at the factory with 20 pounds of R-410A is NOT a Type I appliance (it exceeds 5 lb) — Type II certification is required. Hotel **PTACs (packaged terminal air conditioners)** are Type I if they meet the 5-lb factory-sealed criteria, which most do. A small chest freezer is Type I; a walk-in freezer with field-installed copper lines is Type II.

**Exam Trap:** **The 5-pound limit AND the factory-sealed requirement are BOTH necessary** for Type I classification. A field-charged small system with 4 pounds of refrigerant is NOT Type I — even though it's under 5 lb, it wasn't factory-sealed. Type II certification is required.

**Exam Trap:** A **window air conditioner is Type I.** A **split system (even a "mini-split")** with field-brazed line set is **Type II** — the field connection takes it out of "factory-sealed" status. Don't classify by size or use — classify by factory sealing and charge weight.`,

    "Recovery Standards (90% / 80%)": `Type I recovery equipment must meet specific efficiency standards based on whether the appliance compressor can be operated during recovery. **Recovery with compressor operating** (active recovery): the recovery equipment must achieve **at least 90% recovery efficiency**. **Recovery without compressor operating** (passive recovery, or recovery from a system with a failed compressor): the recovery equipment must achieve **at least 80% recovery efficiency**. The efficiency is measured against the **total refrigerant charge** of the appliance under test conditions specified in EPA's recovery equipment certification protocol.

The technician verifies recovery by reaching the **required vacuum level** per the recovery equipment manufacturer's procedure. Most Type I recovery equipment recovers refrigerant from the appliance and discharges into an **EPA-approved recovery tank** marked with a **yellow top** (color code for HCFC/HFC recovery tanks). The tank must have a **80% maximum fill** (DOT regulation — leaves vapor space for thermal expansion to prevent rupture). Tanks must be **periodically inspected and re-certified** per DOT rules. Refrigerant from Type I recovery may be **returned to the same owner's appliance** after recycling, **sold to a reclamation facility**, or **shipped for disposal/destruction** — but never vented.

**Exam Trap:** **90% with compressor operating; 80% without.** Students remember "90%" and forget the 80% case. A field appliance with a seized compressor can only be passively recovered, so the 80% standard applies. Don't apply 90% to a passive recovery scenario.

**Exam Trap:** **Recovery tank yellow tops** are for recovered HCFC/HFC refrigerant — these are NOT the same as virgin refrigerant tanks (which have different color coding by refrigerant type). A virgin R-410A tank is pink (rose); a recovery tank is gray with yellow top. Mixing virgin and recovered refrigerant in the same tank contaminates both.`,

    "Sales & Disposal Records": `EPA §608 imposes specific record-keeping requirements for refrigerant sales and appliance disposal. **Sales records** for refrigerant in containers **larger than 2 pounds** must be retained showing: (1) the **buyer's name and §608 certification number**, (2) the **quantity sold**, (3) the **refrigerant type**, and (4) the **date of sale**. Records must be kept for **at least 3 years**. The sales restriction means a wholesale distributor cannot sell a 25-lb cylinder of R-410A to an uncertified individual — only to a certified technician with a valid §608 card.

**Disposal records** for small appliances are simpler. The **final person in the disposal chain** (typically a scrap metal recycler, transfer station, or appliance disposal contractor) is responsible for either (a) **recovering the refrigerant** before processing the appliance, or (b) **verifying that recovery has already been performed** (via a signed statement from the previous handler indicating refrigerant was recovered and identifying the recovering technician). **Records of recovery** must show: the date, the appliance description, the quantity of refrigerant recovered, the technician's certification number, and the destination of the recovered refrigerant (recycled, reclaimed, or stored for later disposal).

**Type I "5-pound rule" disposal verification** is simplified: a **signed statement** that the appliance was sent to a properly licensed disposal facility (which itself maintains recovery records) is acceptable for the technician's records. The chain of custody for refrigerant from system to disposal is documented end-to-end.

**Exam Trap:** **3-year record retention** applies to sales records of refrigerant containers larger than 2 lb. Disposal records and technician certifications are typically kept indefinitely. Confusing these retention periods is a common exam error.

**Exam Trap:** The **final person in the disposal chain** is responsible — even if they didn't service the equipment. A scrap metal yard receiving a refrigerator with no recovery documentation is legally on the hook for recovery before crushing or for refusing the appliance. They can't simply say "the previous owner should have recovered it."`
  },

  21: {
    "Recovery Vacuum Levels by Charge": `Type II certification covers **high-pressure appliances** — R-22, R-410A, R-134a, R-404A, R-507A, R-32, R-454B (most modern A/C and commercial refrigeration). EPA §608 requires recovery to a **specified vacuum level** that depends on (a) the **refrigerant being recovered**, (b) the **size of the appliance charge**, and (c) the **age of the recovery equipment** (post-Nov 15, 1993 = stricter standards). Memorize the breakpoint structure — the table itself is open-book on Type II.

**Required vacuum (post-1993 recovery equipment, in Hg vacuum below atmospheric):**

| Refrigerant | < 200 lb charge | ≥ 200 lb charge |
|---|---|---|
| **R-22** (HCFC) | **0 in Hg** (atmospheric) | **10 in Hg vacuum** |
| **R-134a, R-404A, R-507A** (HFC) | **10 in Hg vacuum** | **15 in Hg vacuum** |
| **R-410A** (HFC, very high-press) | **0 in Hg** (atmospheric) | **0 in Hg** (atmospheric) |
| **R-12 (CFC)** legacy | **10 in Hg vacuum** | **15 in Hg vacuum** |
| **R-32, R-454B (A2L)** | follow HFC values (10) | follow HFC values (15) |

Note the counterintuitive **R-410A row**: because R-410A has very high vapor pressure (~400 psig at 110°F), the recovery target is atmospheric — pulling deeper vacuum on R-410A is impractical with field equipment. The 10 / 15 in Hg pattern for most HFCs reflects the trade-off between recovery completeness and recovery time. **Pre-Nov 15, 1993 recovery equipment** is held to a more lenient (less deep) standard but cannot be purchased new today.

**Key Formula:** Required vacuum (Type II, post-1993 equipment) = look up in EPA Table per refrigerant and charge size. Quick rule: **most HFCs at < 200 lb → 10 in Hg; ≥ 200 lb → 15 in Hg. R-22 at < 200 lb → atmospheric. R-410A → always atmospheric.**

**Exam Trap:** **Vacuum levels are EXPRESSED differently in different tables**. **"Inches of mercury vacuum"** (in Hg vacuum) starts at 0 (atmospheric) and increases as vacuum deepens — 25 in Hg vacuum = ~5 in Hg absolute. **"Absolute pressure"** is the actual pressure above zero — 5 in Hg absolute = 25 in Hg vacuum. Confusing these notations will produce wrong answers.

**Exam Trap:** **Larger systems require SHALLOWER vacuum** for recovery, not deeper. Students assume "more refrigerant = need deeper vacuum" — opposite of the actual EPA rule. The rule reflects practical recovery capability with finite recovery time.`,

    "Leak Repair Triggers (10/20/30%)": `Federal §608 leak repair requirements apply to **commercial and industrial refrigeration appliances with a refrigerant charge of 50 pounds or more**. When the **annual leak rate** exceeds the threshold for the appliance type, the owner must **repair the leak within 30 days** (or develop a 1-year retrofit/retirement plan if repair is not feasible). The three thresholds:

- **Industrial process refrigeration**: leak rate threshold **30% per year**
- **Commercial refrigeration** (supermarkets, walk-ins, refrigerated warehouses): leak rate threshold **20% per year**
- **Comfort cooling and all other appliances**: leak rate threshold **10% per year**

The leak rate is calculated as **(pounds added per year ÷ total charge) × 100%**. After repair, a **verification test** must confirm the leak is fixed: an **initial verification** within 30 days of repair, and **follow-up verification** within 30 days after the appliance returns to normal operation. Recordkeeping requirements include the charge size of each appliance over 50 lb, the date and quantity of each refrigerant addition, the calculated leak rate, the repair work performed, and the verification test results.

**Key Formula:** Annual Leak Rate (%) = **(Pounds added in 12 months ÷ Full refrigerant charge) × 100%**. Exceeds **10% (comfort)**, **20% (commercial refrig)**, or **30% (industrial process)** → trigger repair within 30 days.

**Exam Trap:** **Three different thresholds for three appliance categories.** Students remember one number and apply it across the board. **Comfort cooling = 10%**, **commercial refrigeration = 20%**, **industrial process = 30%**. A 15% leak rate on a supermarket walk-in (commercial refrigeration) is BELOW the trigger; a 15% leak rate on a hotel chiller plant (comfort cooling) is ABOVE the trigger.

**Exam Trap:** **Leak repair triggers apply only to systems with 50 lb or more of refrigerant charge.** A residential split system with 4 lb of R-410A is NOT subject to the federal leak repair rule even if it leaks 100% per year. (It IS subject to the venting prohibition during service, but not to the leak repair regulation.) Don't apply the 10/20/30% rule to small systems.`,

    "A2L Refrigerant Handling": `**A2L refrigerants** (R-32, R-454B, R-1234yf, R-466A, etc.) are **mildly flammable** with low burning velocity (≤10 cm/s) and low heat of combustion. They are the new generation of lower-GWP refrigerants replacing R-410A, R-404A, and R-134a as the AIM Act phasedown proceeds. From an EPA §608 perspective, A2L refrigerants are subject to the **same venting prohibition** as A1 refrigerants — but their flammability adds **handling, storage, and equipment requirements** that A1 refrigerants do not have.

**A2L handling requirements**: **No open flames or ignition sources** in the work area during refrigerant transfer or recovery. **Recovery tanks** must be **listed for A2L service** (not all yellow-top tanks are; some are A1-only). **Vacuum pumps** must be A2L-compatible (some standard pumps draw refrigerant into the pump motor where it can ignite). **Brazing on systems with A2L refrigerant requires evacuation to specified low pressure first** to avoid igniting refrigerant during the brazing operation. Ventilation in the work area must prevent refrigerant concentration from reaching the **lower flammability limit (LFL)** — typically ventilation requirements are 4× the airflow needed for A1 work in the same space.

**System charge limits** for A2L vary by application: residential split systems with R-32 are limited to approximately **3.91 lb per circuit** in occupied spaces (varies by room size); commercial systems have similar but larger limits per IEC 60335-2-40 and UL 60335-2-40 referenced by 2018+ IMC. The charge limits ensure that a full release into the smallest occupied space would not reach LFL.

**Exam Trap:** **A2L is "mildly flammable" but NOT non-flammable.** Treating A2L like A1 (no ignition source restrictions, standard recovery tank) is a safety failure that the exam tests. A2L requires its own handling protocols even though §608 venting prohibition is the same.

**Exam Trap:** **System charge limits for A2L vary by room size.** A residential split with R-32 in a small bedroom may exceed the charge limit even at a "normal" capacity — the limit is based on the relationship of charge to room volume, not just absolute capacity. This is one of the testable distinctions between A1 and A2L installation rules.`
  },

  22: {
    "Centrifugal Chiller Operation": `**Centrifugal chillers** are large commercial cooling machines that use a multi-stage centrifugal compressor to compress refrigerant vapor. Classic centrifugal chillers operate with **low-pressure refrigerants** — primarily **R-123 (HCFC)** in older equipment and **R-1233zd (HFO)** in new low-GWP replacements. Low-pressure refrigerants have **boiling points well below 0°F** and operate at **sub-atmospheric pressures throughout much of the cycle** — the evaporator typically runs at **5 to 12 inches Hg vacuum** (8 to 11 psia absolute, below atmospheric pressure). This sub-atmospheric operation has critical implications for service and recovery.

A hotel chiller plant with **R-123 centrifugal chillers** is classified as a **Class B1 refrigerant** application (toxic but non-flammable). Machinery room requirements per IMC 1105 fully apply — emergency switch outside, refrigerant detector at appropriate location, emergency ventilation, separation from occupied space. The chiller has a **purge unit** that periodically removes non-condensable gases (air and water vapor) that accumulate in the condenser due to **air leaking inward through sub-atmospheric seals** — this is a built-in characteristic of low-pressure operation. The purge collects refrigerant for return to the system and discharges only the air/water — this is **NOT a §608 violation** because it's air being purged, not refrigerant.

**Exam Trap:** **R-123 centrifugal chillers operate under vacuum on the low side.** Air leaks INWARD into the system (not out) — opposite of high-pressure systems. The purge unit is a normal part of operation, not a sign of a leak.

**Exam Trap:** **R-123 is being phased out** under the Montreal Protocol HCFC phasedown. New equipment uses R-1233zd (HFO replacement) or other low-pressure alternatives. Existing R-123 chillers can continue to operate, but new R-123 production has ended — service refrigerant must come from reclaimed stock. Plan retrofit/replacement before refrigerant becomes unavailable.`,

    "25 mm Hg Absolute Recovery": `**Type III certification** under EPA §608 covers **low-pressure appliances** — primarily centrifugal chillers using R-123, R-1233zd, R-11 (in older equipment), and similar low-pressure refrigerants. The required recovery vacuum for Type III systems (post-November 1993 recovery equipment) is **25 mm Hg absolute pressure** — equivalent to about **29 inches Hg vacuum** (very deep vacuum, nearly complete removal of refrigerant vapor). This is dramatically deeper than the Type II vacuum requirements because low-pressure refrigerants have such low boiling points that they will continue to evaporate from oil and surfaces at higher absolute pressures.

**25 mm Hg absolute** is a difficult vacuum to achieve and verify. The technician uses an **electronic micron gauge** for measurement (mechanical vacuum gauges are not accurate at this level — they read everything below ~29 in Hg as "fully vacuumed"). The recovery process can take **8 to 24 hours or more** for a large chiller because the refrigerant boils off the oil and tube surfaces slowly at low pressure. **Recovery cylinders** for low-pressure refrigerant must be **rated for vacuum** (a standard high-pressure cylinder is not vacuum-rated and can implode under deep vacuum).

**Key Formula:** Required vacuum for Type III recovery (post-1993 equipment) = **25 mm Hg absolute** ≈ **29 in Hg vacuum** ≈ **1 in Hg absolute**.

**Exam Trap:** **25 mm Hg absolute = ~29 in Hg vacuum** — these are the same vacuum expressed in different units. Don't confuse absolute pressure (small number, close to zero = deep vacuum) with vacuum below atmospheric (large number = deep vacuum). The exam will sometimes give you the value in one unit and ask if it satisfies a requirement stated in the other.

**Exam Trap:** **Recovery from low-pressure systems takes much longer** than from high-pressure systems. A technician who shuts off recovery after 2 hours on a centrifugal chiller has not met the §608 requirement — the system probably still has significant refrigerant in the oil and on internal surfaces. Continue until the micron gauge holds the required vacuum for the specified time (typically 5–15 minutes after isolation).`,

    "Purge Units & Pump-Out": `**Purge units** on low-pressure chillers continuously or periodically remove **non-condensable gases (air and water vapor)** from the condenser. Because the evaporator and parts of the system operate under vacuum, **air leaks INTO the system** through any seal imperfection. Air accumulates in the condenser, raising condensing pressure, reducing efficiency, and (with water vapor present) forming corrosive acids that attack the refrigerant and internal components. The purge unit cools a sample of condenser gas to a temperature below the refrigerant condensing point — the refrigerant condenses and drains back to the system, while non-condensables (which don't condense at that temperature) are vented to atmosphere.

Modern **high-efficiency purge units** also collect any small amount of refrigerant that escapes with the air, condense it in a secondary heat exchanger, and return it — releasing only the actual non-condensables. **Purge unit run-time logs** are an excellent diagnostic for chiller plant health: an increasing purge run-time over weeks/months indicates an increasing air infiltration rate, signaling a leak that needs to be found and repaired. EPA §608 **leak repair rules apply** to centrifugal chillers ≥ 50 lb charge (most are, easily) — the **10% per year leak threshold for comfort cooling** triggers required repair within 30 days when exceeded.

**Pump-out (refrigerant transfer)** is the process of moving the chiller's refrigerant charge from the evaporator (or main shell) to a separate **storage vessel** for service work. Pump-out is typically done before opening the system for major service to avoid full recovery. The storage vessel is a **separate ASME-rated receiver** sized for the full chiller charge plus expansion margin. Pump-out is performed by reversing the chiller's normal cycle (using the system's own compressor and condenser) and trapping the refrigerant in the storage vessel until service is complete, then **pumping it back** when reassembly is complete.

**Exam Trap:** **Purge unit operation is NOT a §608 venting violation** even though it releases gases to atmosphere. Modern high-efficiency purges release only **non-condensables (air, water vapor)** — refrigerant is recaptured and returned. Older inefficient purges that vent some refrigerant ARE a §608 issue and are being phased out.

**Exam Trap:** **Pump-out and recovery are different operations.** Pump-out uses the chiller's own components to move refrigerant within the same system to a storage vessel — no external recovery machine needed. Recovery uses external equipment to permanently remove refrigerant for storage, recycling, reclamation, or disposal. Confusing these on the exam will lead to wrong procedural answers.`
  }
};
